export const content = {"00-overview":"# DevOps Mastery Plan\n\n> **Schedule:** 2 hours/day | **Duration:** 9 weeks (~63 sessions) | **Support:** Self-directed, with structured self-review checkpoints\n\n## How to Use This Guide\n\n1. Follow the phases in order — each phase builds on the previous\n2. Each session = 45 min reading + 60 min hands-on + 15 min notes/questions\n3. Use the checkboxes below to track your progress\n4. Run through the self-review checklist at each weekly review\n\n## Progress Tracker\n\n### Phase 1 — Docker (Weeks 1–2)\n- [ ] Day 01 — Containers & Images basics\n- [ ] Day 02 — Writing Dockerfiles\n- [ ] Day 03 — Layers, caching, .dockerignore\n- [ ] Day 04 — Volumes and bind mounts\n- [ ] Day 05 — Docker networking\n- [ ] Day 06 — Docker Compose\n- [ ] Day 07 — Multi-stage builds\n- [ ] Day 08 — Azure Container Registry (ACR)\n- [ ] Day 09 — Environment variables and secrets\n- [ ] Day 10 — Health checks and resource limits\n- [ ] Day 11–14 — Mini-project + self-review checklist\n\n### Phase 2 — Azure Core Services (Weeks 3–4)\n- [ ] Day 15 — Azure portal, resource groups, subscriptions\n- [ ] Day 16 — Azure App Service\n- [ ] Day 17 — Azure Container Apps\n- [ ] Day 18 — Azure Key Vault\n- [ ] Day 19 — Virtual Networks and NSGs\n- [ ] Day 20 — Azure Blob Storage\n- [ ] Day 21 — Architecture self-review\n- [ ] Day 22 — Azure Communication Services intro\n- [ ] Day 23 — Sending email via ACS SDK\n- [ ] Day 24 — Email templates and error handling\n- [ ] Day 25 — Microsoft Entra ID fundamentals\n- [ ] Day 26 — OAuth 2.0 / OIDC flows\n- [ ] Day 27 — RBAC and app roles\n- [ ] Day 28 — Auth + email demo review\n\n### Phase 3 — Deploy Database (Week 5)\n- [ ] Day 29 — Choose the right Azure database\n- [ ] Day 30 — Provision and connect\n- [ ] Day 31 — Managed Identity auth\n- [ ] Day 32 — Database migrations in CI/CD\n- [ ] Day 33 — Backups and restore\n- [ ] Day 34 — Monitoring and alerts\n- [ ] Day 35 — Self-review checkpoint\n\n### Phase 4 — Azure CI/CD (Weeks 6–8)\n- [ ] Day 36 — Azure DevOps overview\n- [ ] Day 37 — YAML pipeline basics\n- [ ] Day 38 — Pipeline agents\n- [ ] Day 39 — Variables, variable groups, secrets\n- [ ] Day 40 — Artifacts\n- [ ] Day 41 — Pipeline templates\n- [ ] Day 42 — Build pipeline review\n- [ ] Day 43 — Environments and approvals\n- [ ] Day 44 — Deploy to App Service\n- [ ] Day 45 — Deploy to Container Apps\n- [ ] Day 46 — DB migrations in pipeline\n- [ ] Day 47 — Deployment strategies\n- [ ] Day 48 — Rollback strategy\n- [ ] Day 49 — Full CD pipeline review\n- [ ] Day 50 — Unit tests in pipeline\n- [ ] Day 51 — Static analysis and SAST\n- [ ] Day 52 — Application Insights\n- [ ] Day 53 — Log Analytics and KQL\n- [ ] Day 54 — Dashboards and alert rules\n- [ ] Day 55 — Cost management\n- [ ] Day 56 — Monitoring review\n\n### Phase 5 — Capstone Project (Week 9)\n- [ ] Day 57–59 — Build full system\n- [ ] Day 60–61 — Pipeline testing and break/fix\n- [ ] Day 62 — Security review\n- [ ] Day 63 — Final self-review and demo\n\n---\n\n## Sub-Pages\n\n| # | Topic | Link |\n|---|-------|------|\n| 1 | Docker | [01-docker/README.md](01-docker/README.md) |\n| 2 | Azure Core Services | [02-azure-core-services/README.md](02-azure-core-services/README.md) |\n| 3 | Azure Email (ACS) | [03-azure-email/README.md](03-azure-email/README.md) |\n| 4 | Azure Authentication (Entra ID) | [04-azure-authentication/README.md](04-azure-authentication/README.md) |\n| 5 | Deploy Database | [05-deploy-database/README.md](05-deploy-database/README.md) |\n| 6 | Azure CI/CD | [06-azure-cicd/README.md](06-azure-cicd/README.md) |\n| 7 | Capstone Project | [07-capstone/README.md](07-capstone/README.md) |\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n### Tools to Install\n\n```powershell\n# Docker Desktop\n# Download from: https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/\n\n# Azure CLI\nwinget install Microsoft.AzureCLI\n\n# Azure DevOps CLI extension\naz extension add --name azure-devops\n\n# Git\nwinget install Git.Git\n\n# VS Code extensions (install from VS Code)\n# - Docker\n# - Azure Tools\n# - Azure Pipelines\n```\n\n### Accounts to Create\n\n- [ ] [Azure Free Account](https://azure.microsoft.com/free/) — $200 credit for 30 days\n- [ ] [Azure DevOps](https://dev.azure.com/) — free for 5 users\n- [ ] [Docker Hub](https://hub.docker.com/) — free tier\n\n### Verify Setup\n\n```powershell\ndocker --version          # Docker version 24+\naz --version              # azure-cli 2.50+\ngit --version             # git 2.40+\n```\n\n---\n\n## Key Concepts Map\n\n```\nYour App Code\n    |\n    v\n[Docker Image] ──push──> [Azure Container Registry]\n    |                              |\n    v                              v\n[Docker Compose]         [Azure Container Apps / App Service]\n(local dev)                        |\n                          [Azure Key Vault] (secrets)\n                          [Azure SQL / PostgreSQL] (data)\n                          [Azure Blob Storage] (files)\n                          [Entra ID] (auth)\n                          [ACS] (email)\n                                   ^\n                                   |\n                    [Azure DevOps Pipelines] (automates all of this)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Weekly Review Template\n\nCopy this into your notes at the end of each week:\n\n```\nWeek: ___\nTopics covered: ___\n\n3 things I learned:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n\n1 thing I'm still unclear on:\n-\n\nOpen questions to research further:\n1.\n2.\n```\n","01-docker":"# Phase 1 — Docker\n\n> **Duration:** Days 1–14 (2 weeks) | **Goal:** Build, run, and compose containerized apps\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [What is Docker?](#1-what-is-docker)\n2. [Installation](#2-installation)\n3. [Core Concepts](#3-core-concepts)\n4. [Writing Dockerfiles](#4-writing-dockerfiles)\n5. [Essential Docker Commands](#5-essential-docker-commands)\n6. [Volumes and Bind Mounts](#6-volumes-and-bind-mounts)\n7. [Docker Networking](#7-docker-networking)\n8. [Docker Compose](#8-docker-compose)\n9. [Multi-stage Builds](#9-multi-stage-builds)\n10. [Azure Container Registry (ACR)](#10-azure-container-registry-acr)\n11. [Health Checks and Resource Limits](#11-health-checks-and-resource-limits)\n12. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#12-day-by-day-exercises)\n13. [Mini-Project](#13-mini-project)\n14. [Cheat Sheet](#14-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. What is Docker?\n\nDocker solves the \"works on my machine\" problem. It packages your application and **all its dependencies** into a single portable unit called a **container**.\n\n### Virtual Machine vs Container\n\n```\nVirtual Machine                     Container\n┌──────────────────┐                ┌──────────────────┐\n│   Your App       │                │   Your App       │\n│   Libraries      │                │   Libraries      │\n│   Guest OS       │                ├──────────────────┤\n│   Hypervisor     │                │  Container Runtime│\n│   Host OS        │                │  Host OS         │\n│   Hardware       │                │  Hardware        │\n└──────────────────┘                └──────────────────┘\n   Heavy (~GBs)                        Light (~MBs)\n   Slow to start                       Starts in seconds\n   Full OS isolation                   Process-level isolation\n```\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Docker Engine** — the daemon that runs on your machine\n- **Docker CLI** — the `docker` command you type\n- **Docker Image** — a read-only template (like a class in OOP)\n- **Docker Container** — a running instance of an image (like an object)\n- **Docker Hub / Registry** — stores and distributes images\n- **Dockerfile** — instructions to build an image\n- **Docker Compose** — defines multi-container applications\n\n---\n\n## 2. Installation\n\n### Windows (Docker Desktop)\n\n1. Download Docker Desktop from [docker.com](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/)\n2. Install and enable WSL 2 integration when prompted\n3. Start Docker Desktop\n\n```powershell\n# Verify installation\ndocker --version\ndocker run hello-world\n```\n\nExpected output from `hello-world`:\n```\nHello from Docker!\nThis message shows that your installation appears to be working correctly.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core Concepts\n\n### Images\n\nAn image is a **layered, read-only filesystem**. Think of it as a recipe.\n\n```powershell\n# Pull an image from Docker Hub\ndocker pull nginx\n\n# List images on your machine\ndocker images\n\n# Inspect an image's layers\ndocker history nginx\n\n# Remove an image\ndocker rmi nginx\n```\n\n### Containers\n\nA container is a **running instance** of an image. Think of it as a dish made from the recipe.\n\n```viz\ncontainer-lifecycle\n```\n\n```powershell\n# Run a container\ndocker run nginx\n\n# Run in background (detached)\ndocker run -d nginx\n\n# Run with a name\ndocker run -d --name my-nginx nginx\n\n# List running containers\ndocker ps\n\n# List ALL containers (including stopped)\ndocker ps -a\n\n# Stop a container\ndocker stop my-nginx\n\n# Remove a container\ndocker rm my-nginx\n\n# Stop and remove in one go\ndocker rm -f my-nginx\n```\n\n### Image Layers\n\nEvery instruction in a Dockerfile creates a new **layer**. Layers are cached and reused.\n\n```\nLayer 5: COPY . /app          <- your code (changes often)\nLayer 4: RUN npm install       <- dependencies (changes less often)\nLayer 3: WORKDIR /app\nLayer 2: RUN apt-get update\nLayer 1: FROM node:18          <- base OS + runtime (rarely changes)\n```\n\n**Rule:** Put things that change frequently at the **bottom** of your Dockerfile so upper layers are cached.\n\n```viz\nlayer-cache\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Writing Dockerfiles\n\n### Dockerfile Instructions Reference\n\n| Instruction | Purpose | Example |\n|------------|---------|---------|\n| `FROM` | Base image | `FROM node:18-alpine` |\n| `WORKDIR` | Set working directory | `WORKDIR /app` |\n| `COPY` | Copy files from host | `COPY package.json .` |\n| `ADD` | Like COPY + can untar/URL | `ADD archive.tar.gz /app` |\n| `RUN` | Execute command during build | `RUN npm install` |\n| `CMD` | Default command at runtime | `CMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]` |\n| `ENTRYPOINT` | Fixed executable | `ENTRYPOINT [\"dotnet\"]` |\n| `ENV` | Set environment variable | `ENV NODE_ENV=production` |\n| `EXPOSE` | Document which port app uses | `EXPOSE 3000` |\n| `ARG` | Build-time variable | `ARG VERSION=1.0` |\n| `VOLUME` | Declare mount point | `VOLUME /data` |\n| `USER` | Run as specific user | `USER node` |\n\n### Example 1 — Node.js App\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:18-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Copy package files first (layer caching trick)\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci --only=production\n\n# Copy source AFTER installing deps\nCOPY . .\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\n# Use array form (exec form) — avoids shell interpretation\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\n### Example 2 — ASP.NET Core App\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine AS base\nWORKDIR /app\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY [\"MyApp.csproj\", \".\"]\nRUN dotnet restore\nCOPY . .\nRUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app/publish\n\nFROM base AS final\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /app/publish .\nENTRYPOINT [\"dotnet\", \"MyApp.dll\"]\n```\n\n### .dockerignore\n\nCreate `.dockerignore` next to your Dockerfile to exclude files from the build context:\n\n```\nnode_modules\n.git\n.env\n*.log\ndist\ncoverage\n.dockerignore\nDockerfile\n```\n\n### Build Commands\n\n```powershell\n# Build an image\ndocker build -t my-app:latest .\n\n# Build with a specific Dockerfile\ndocker build -f Dockerfile.prod -t my-app:prod .\n\n# Build with a build argument\ndocker build --build-arg VERSION=2.0 -t my-app:2.0 .\n\n# View build cache usage\ndocker system df\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Essential Docker Commands\n\n### Container Lifecycle\n\n```powershell\n# Run and immediately enter shell\ndocker run -it ubuntu /bin/bash\n\n# Run with port mapping (host:container)\ndocker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx\n# Now visit http://localhost:8080\n\n# Run with environment variable\ndocker run -d -e DATABASE_URL=\"postgres://...\" my-app\n\n# Execute command in running container\ndocker exec -it my-nginx /bin/bash\n\n# View container logs\ndocker logs my-nginx\ndocker logs -f my-nginx        # follow (like tail -f)\ndocker logs --tail 50 my-nginx # last 50 lines\n\n# Copy files to/from container\ndocker cp ./file.txt my-nginx:/app/file.txt\ndocker cp my-nginx:/app/log.txt ./log.txt\n\n# Inspect container details (IP, env vars, mounts, etc.)\ndocker inspect my-nginx\n\n# View resource usage\ndocker stats\n```\n\n### Cleanup\n\n```powershell\n# Remove all stopped containers\ndocker container prune\n\n# Remove unused images\ndocker image prune\n\n# Remove everything unused (containers, images, networks, cache)\ndocker system prune -a\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Volumes and Bind Mounts\n\n### Why Volumes?\n\nContainer filesystems are **ephemeral** — when a container is removed, all data inside it is lost. Volumes persist data outside the container.\n\n### Types\n\n| Type | Use case | Path |\n|------|----------|------|\n| **Named volume** | Databases, persistent data | Managed by Docker |\n| **Bind mount** | Development (live code reload) | Your host filesystem |\n| **tmpfs** | Sensitive temp data | Memory only |\n\n### Named Volumes\n\n```powershell\n# Create a volume\ndocker volume create my-data\n\n# Run container with volume\ndocker run -d -v my-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16\n\n# List volumes\ndocker volume ls\n\n# Inspect a volume (find where Docker stores it)\ndocker volume inspect my-data\n\n# Remove volume\ndocker volume rm my-data\n```\n\n### Bind Mounts (Development)\n\n```powershell\n# Mount current directory into container\n# Changes on host are reflected immediately in container\ndocker run -d -v ${PWD}:/app -p 3000:3000 my-node-app\n\n# Windows PowerShell version\ndocker run -d -v \"$(pwd):/app\" -p 3000:3000 my-node-app\n```\n\n### When to use which\n\n- **Named volumes** — production data, databases\n- **Bind mounts** — local development, hot reload\n- Never use bind mounts in production containers\n\n---\n\n## 7. Docker Networking\n\n### Default Networks\n\n```powershell\n# List networks\ndocker network ls\n\n# Default networks:\n# bridge  — default for containers on the same host\n# host    — container shares the host network stack\n# none    — no networking\n```\n\n### Bridge Network (Default)\n\nContainers on the default bridge can only communicate via **IP address** (not name).\n\n```powershell\ndocker run -d --name app1 nginx\ndocker run -d --name app2 nginx\n\n# app2 cannot reach app1 by name on the default bridge\n```\n\n### User-Defined Bridge Network\n\nContainers on a **user-defined bridge** can reach each other by **container name** — this is what you want.\n\n```powershell\n# Create a custom network\ndocker network create my-network\n\n# Run containers on that network\ndocker run -d --name db --network my-network postgres:16\ndocker run -d --name api --network my-network my-api-app\n\n# Now \"api\" can connect to \"db\" using hostname \"db\"\n# Connection string: postgres://user:pass@db:5432/mydb\n```\n\n### Port Mapping\n\n```powershell\n# -p host_port:container_port\ndocker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx        # host:8080 -> container:80\ndocker run -d -p 5432:5432 postgres   # expose postgres to host\ndocker run -d -P nginx                 # auto-assign host ports\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Docker Compose\n\nDocker Compose defines **multi-container applications** in a single YAML file.\n\n```viz\ncompose-stack\n```\n\n### Basic Structure\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml\nversion: \"3.9\"\n\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"3000:3000\"\n    environment:\n      - DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@db:5432/mydb\n    depends_on:\n      db:\n        condition: service_healthy\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass\n      POSTGRES_DB: mydb\n    volumes:\n      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U user -d mydb\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres_data:\n```\n\n### Compose Commands\n\n```powershell\n# Start all services (build if needed)\ndocker compose up\n\n# Start in background\ndocker compose up -d\n\n# Rebuild images then start\ndocker compose up --build\n\n# Stop all services (keeps containers)\ndocker compose stop\n\n# Stop and remove containers\ndocker compose down\n\n# Stop, remove containers AND volumes\ndocker compose down -v\n\n# View logs for all services\ndocker compose logs -f\n\n# View logs for one service\ndocker compose logs -f api\n\n# Run a one-off command in a service\ndocker compose exec api sh\n\n# Scale a service\ndocker compose up -d --scale api=3\n```\n\n### Using .env with Compose\n\n```env\n# .env file (same directory as docker-compose.yml)\nPOSTGRES_USER=myuser\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret123\nPOSTGRES_DB=myapp\n```\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml — reference .env variables\nservices:\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: ${POSTGRES_USER}\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}\n      POSTGRES_DB: ${POSTGRES_DB}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Multi-stage Builds\n\nMulti-stage builds let you use one stage to **build** and another to **run**, keeping the final image small.\n\n```viz\nmultistage\n```\n\n### Why it matters\n\n```\nWithout multi-stage:  node:18 image + dev dependencies + source = ~1.2 GB\nWith multi-stage:     node:18-alpine + only production deps = ~120 MB\n```\n\n### Node.js Example\n\n```dockerfile\n# Stage 1: Build\nFROM node:18 AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build        # compile TypeScript, bundle, etc.\n\n# Stage 2: Production\nFROM node:18-alpine AS production\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci --only=production\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist  # only copy compiled output\nEXPOSE 3000\nUSER node                              # run as non-root\nCMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n### .NET Example\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY . .\nRUN dotnet restore\nRUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /publish\n\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine AS final\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /publish .\nUSER app\nENTRYPOINT [\"dotnet\", \"MyApp.dll\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Azure Container Registry (ACR)\n\nACR is a private Docker registry hosted on Azure.\n\n### Create ACR\n\n```powershell\n# Login to Azure\naz login\n\n# Create a resource group\naz group create --name rg-devops-learning --location eastus\n\n# Create ACR (name must be globally unique, alphanumeric only)\naz acr create --resource-group rg-devops-learning --name mydevopsacr --sku Basic\n\n# Login to ACR\naz acr login --name mydevopsacr\n```\n\n### Push Image to ACR\n\n```powershell\n# Get ACR login server URL\naz acr show --name mydevopsacr --query loginServer --output tsv\n# Output: mydevopsacr.azurecr.io\n\n# Tag your local image with the ACR server\ndocker tag my-app:latest mydevopsacr.azurecr.io/my-app:latest\n\n# Push to ACR\ndocker push mydevopsacr.azurecr.io/my-app:latest\n\n# List images in ACR\naz acr repository list --name mydevopsacr --output table\n\n# Pull from ACR\ndocker pull mydevopsacr.azurecr.io/my-app:latest\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Health Checks and Resource Limits\n\n### Health Checks\n\n```dockerfile\n# In Dockerfile\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=10s --start-period=5s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1\n```\n\n```yaml\n# In docker-compose.yml\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: my-app\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost:3000/health\"]\n      interval: 30s\n      timeout: 10s\n      retries: 3\n      start_period: 10s\n```\n\n```powershell\n# Check health status\ndocker inspect --format='{{.State.Health.Status}}' my-container\n```\n\n### Resource Limits\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml\nservices:\n  api:\n    image: my-app\n    deploy:\n      resources:\n        limits:\n          cpus: \"0.5\"       # max 50% of one CPU core\n          memory: 512M      # max 512 MB RAM\n        reservations:\n          cpus: \"0.25\"\n          memory: 256M\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 1 — Hello Docker\n```powershell\ndocker run hello-world\ndocker run -it ubuntu bash             # enter a container shell\ndocker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx         # run nginx, visit localhost:8080\ndocker ps\ndocker ps -a\ndocker stop <container_id>\ndocker rm <container_id>\n```\n\n### Day 2 — Your First Dockerfile\nCreate a simple web server in any language and write a Dockerfile for it. Build and run it:\n```powershell\ndocker build -t my-first-app .\ndocker run -d -p 3000:3000 my-first-app\n# Visit http://localhost:3000\n```\n\n### Day 3 — Understand Caching\n1. Build your image, note the time\n2. Change only your source code (not package.json)\n3. Build again — observe which layers are cached\n4. Now change package.json\n5. Build again — observe the cache breaks at `npm install`\n\n### Day 4 — Volumes\n```powershell\n# Run postgres with a named volume\ndocker run -d --name pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16\n\n# Connect and create a database\ndocker exec -it pg psql -U postgres -c \"CREATE DATABASE testdb;\"\n\n# Stop and remove the container\ndocker rm -f pg\n\n# Run a NEW postgres container with the SAME volume\ndocker run -d --name pg2 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16\n\n# The database should still exist\ndocker exec -it pg2 psql -U postgres -c \"\\l\"\n```\n\n### Day 5 — Networking\n```powershell\n# Create a network\ndocker network create lab-network\n\n# Run two containers on it\ndocker run -d --name server --network lab-network nginx\ndocker run -it --name client --network lab-network alpine sh\n\n# Inside the alpine shell, ping and curl the nginx container by NAME\nping server\nwget -qO- http://server\n```\n\n### Day 6 — Docker Compose\nBuild a Compose file with your app + PostgreSQL. Run `docker compose up` and verify the app connects to the database.\n\n### Day 7 — Multi-stage Build\nMeasure the image size before and after applying multi-stage builds:\n```powershell\ndocker images --format \"table {{.Repository}}\\t{{.Tag}}\\t{{.Size}}\"\n```\n\n### Day 8 — Push to ACR\nFollow Section 10 to push your image to Azure Container Registry.\n\n### Day 9 — Env Vars\nMove all hardcoded values (passwords, ports, URLs) from your code into environment variables. Use `.env` file locally and pass via `-e` or Compose.\n\n### Day 10 — Health Check\nAdd a `/health` endpoint to your app and configure a Docker health check. Verify with `docker inspect`.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Mini-Project (Days 11–14)\n\nBuild a fully containerized system:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ docker-compose.yml                                   │\n│                                                      │\n│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐   │\n│  │  nginx   │───>│   api    │───>│  postgres    │   │\n│  │ (proxy)  │    │  :3000   │    │    :5432     │   │\n│  │  :80     │    │          │    │              │   │\n│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘   │\n│                                         │            │\n│                                  named volume        │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nRequirements:\n- [ ] Nginx acts as reverse proxy to your API\n- [ ] API has at least one endpoint that reads/writes to PostgreSQL\n- [ ] Multi-stage Dockerfile for the API\n- [ ] `.env` file for all config\n- [ ] Health check on the API\n- [ ] Named volume for PostgreSQL data\n- [ ] `docker compose up` starts everything in the right order\n- [ ] Data persists after `docker compose down` and `docker compose up`\n- [ ] Image pushed to ACR\n\n**Self-review checklist:**\n- Image size is reasonable (< 200MB for most apps)\n- No secrets in the Dockerfile or image\n- Non-root user in production image\n- Health check passes\n\n---\n\n## 14. Cheat Sheet\n\n```powershell\n# BUILD\ndocker build -t name:tag .\ndocker build --no-cache -t name:tag .\n\n# RUN\ndocker run -d -p host:container --name name image\ndocker run -it image /bin/sh\ndocker run --rm image command          # remove container when done\n\n# MANAGE CONTAINERS\ndocker ps / docker ps -a\ndocker stop name\ndocker rm name\ndocker rm -f name                      # force stop + remove\ndocker logs -f name\ndocker exec -it name sh\ndocker inspect name\n\n# MANAGE IMAGES\ndocker images\ndocker pull image:tag\ndocker rmi image:tag\ndocker tag source target\ndocker push target\n\n# VOLUMES\ndocker volume ls / create / rm / inspect\n\n# NETWORKS\ndocker network ls / create / inspect\ndocker network connect network container\n\n# COMPOSE\ndocker compose up -d\ndocker compose down\ndocker compose down -v                 # also remove volumes\ndocker compose logs -f service\ndocker compose exec service sh\ndocker compose build\n\n# CLEANUP\ndocker system prune -a                 # remove everything unused\ndocker container prune\ndocker image prune\ndocker volume prune\n```\n","02-azure-core-services":"# Phase 2 — Azure Core Services\n\n> **Duration:** Days 15–21 (Week 3) | **Goal:** Deploy a server, manage secrets, store files, set up networking\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Azure Fundamentals](#1-azure-fundamentals)\n2. [Azure CLI Basics](#2-azure-cli-basics)\n3. [Azure App Service](#3-azure-app-service)\n4. [Azure Container Apps](#4-azure-container-apps)\n5. [Azure Key Vault](#5-azure-key-vault)\n6. [Azure Virtual Networks](#6-azure-virtual-networks)\n7. [Azure Blob Storage](#7-azure-blob-storage)\n8. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#8-day-by-day-exercises)\n9. [Cheat Sheet](#9-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Azure Fundamentals\n\n### Core Hierarchy\n\n```\nAzure Account (your login)\n└── Subscription (billing boundary — you can have many)\n    └── Resource Group (logical container for related resources)\n        ├── App Service\n        ├── SQL Database\n        ├── Key Vault\n        └── Storage Account\n```\n\n```viz\nazure-hierarchy\n```\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Concept | What it is | Analogy |\n|---------|-----------|---------|\n| **Subscription** | Billing unit — all costs roll up here | Your bank account |\n| **Resource Group** | Logical grouping of related resources | A project folder |\n| **Region** | Physical data center location (East US, West Europe, etc.) | Physical server location |\n| **Resource** | Any Azure service you create | A file in the folder |\n| **SKU / Tier** | Pricing and capability tier | Basic, Standard, Premium |\n| **Tags** | Key-value metadata on any resource | Labels for organization |\n\n### Resource Naming Convention\n\nUse a consistent naming pattern so you always know what a resource is:\n\n```\n{resource-type}-{app-name}-{environment}-{region}\n\nExamples:\nrg-myapp-dev-eastus          (resource group)\napp-myapp-dev                (app service)\nkv-myapp-dev                 (key vault)\nacr-myapp-prod               (container registry)\nsql-myapp-prod               (sql server)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Azure CLI Basics\n\nThe Azure CLI (`az`) is the most efficient way to interact with Azure — faster than the portal for learning.\n\n### Authentication\n\n```powershell\n# Login interactively\naz login\n\n# Login with a service principal (CI/CD)\naz login --service-principal -u <app-id> -p <secret> --tenant <tenant-id>\n\n# See who you're logged in as\naz account show\n\n# List your subscriptions\naz account list --output table\n\n# Set a specific subscription as default\naz account set --subscription \"My Subscription Name\"\n```\n\n### Essential Commands\n\n```powershell\n# Resource groups\naz group create --name rg-learning --location eastus\naz group list --output table\naz group delete --name rg-learning --yes --no-wait\n\n# Find resource types\naz provider list --query \"[?registrationState=='Registered']\" --output table\n\n# Get help for any command\naz appservice --help\naz webapp create --help\n\n# Query output with JMESPath\naz account show --query \"name\" --output tsv\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Azure App Service\n\nApp Service is a **fully managed platform** for hosting web apps, APIs, and backends — you don't manage the VM.\n\n### App Service Plan vs App Service\n\n```\nApp Service Plan = the virtual machine (you pay for this)\nApp Service      = your application running on that plan (free to create, costs come from the plan)\n\nOne plan can host multiple apps.\n```\n\n```viz\nappservice-plan\n```\n\n### Pricing Tiers\n\n| Tier | Use case | Features |\n|------|----------|----------|\n| Free (F1) | Learning | 60 min CPU/day, shared, no custom domain |\n| Basic (B1) | Dev/test | Dedicated, custom domain, manual scale |\n| Standard (S1) | Production | Auto-scale, deployment slots, backups |\n| Premium (P1v3) | High scale | More memory, VNet integration |\n\n### Create and Deploy\n\n```powershell\n# Create App Service Plan\naz appservice plan create \\\n  --name plan-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --sku B1 \\\n  --is-linux\n\n# Create Web App (for containers)\naz webapp create \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --plan plan-myapp-dev \\\n  --deployment-container-image-name nginx\n\n# Deploy your Docker image from ACR\naz webapp config container set \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --docker-custom-image-name myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:latest \\\n  --docker-registry-server-url https://myacr.azurecr.io\n\n# Enable ACR access (managed identity)\naz webapp identity assign --name app-myapp-dev --resource-group rg-learning\naz acr update -n myacr --admin-enabled true\n\n# View app URL\naz webapp show --name app-myapp-dev --resource-group rg-learning --query defaultHostName --output tsv\n\n# Stream logs\naz webapp log tail --name app-myapp-dev --resource-group rg-learning\n\n# Restart app\naz webapp restart --name app-myapp-dev --resource-group rg-learning\n```\n\n### Environment Variables (App Settings)\n\n```powershell\n# Set an app setting (environment variable)\naz webapp config appsettings set \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --settings DATABASE_URL=\"postgres://...\" NODE_ENV=\"production\"\n\n# List all settings\naz webapp config appsettings list --name app-myapp-dev --resource-group rg-learning\n\n# Delete a setting\naz webapp config appsettings delete \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --setting-names DATABASE_URL\n```\n\n### Deployment Slots\n\nSlots let you run staging and production side by side, then swap:\n\n```powershell\n# Create a staging slot\naz webapp deployment slot create \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --slot staging\n\n# Swap staging -> production\naz webapp deployment slot swap \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --slot staging\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Azure Container Apps\n\nContainer Apps is the **modern way** to run containers on Azure. It's serverless, scales to zero, and handles HTTPS/ingress for you.\n\n### vs App Service\n\n| | App Service | Container Apps |\n|--|------------|----------------|\n| Billing | Per plan (VM always on) | Per request + CPU/memory used |\n| Scale to zero | No (Basic/Standard) | Yes |\n| Microservices | Awkward | Native |\n| Complexity | Lower | Medium |\n| Best for | Single web apps | APIs, microservices, background jobs |\n\n### Create Container App\n\n```powershell\n# Install extension\naz extension add --name containerapp\naz provider register --namespace Microsoft.App\n\n# Create a Container Apps Environment (shared infrastructure)\naz containerapp env create \\\n  --name env-learning \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus\n\n# Deploy a container app\naz containerapp create \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --environment env-learning \\\n  --image myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:latest \\\n  --registry-server myacr.azurecr.io \\\n  --target-port 3000 \\\n  --ingress external \\\n  --min-replicas 0 \\\n  --max-replicas 10\n\n# Get the app URL\naz containerapp show \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn \\\n  --output tsv\n\n# Update to a new image version\naz containerapp update \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --image myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:v2\n```\n\n### Environment Variables\n\n```powershell\naz containerapp update \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --set-env-vars \"NODE_ENV=production\" \"PORT=3000\"\n```\n\n### Scaling Rules\n\n```powershell\n# Scale based on HTTP traffic\naz containerapp update \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --scale-rule-name http-rule \\\n  --scale-rule-type http \\\n  --scale-rule-http-concurrency 100\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Azure Key Vault\n\nKey Vault is a **secure store** for secrets, certificates, and keys. Never hardcode secrets in code or environment variables directly — store them in Key Vault.\n\n### Why Key Vault?\n\n```\nWithout Key Vault:                    With Key Vault:\n.env file → in source control risk    KV stores secret\nApp config → visible to all devs      App references KV secret by name\nRotation requires code change         Rotate secret in KV, app gets new value\n```\n\n### Create Key Vault\n\n```powershell\n# Create Key Vault\naz keyvault create \\\n  --name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus\n\n# Add a secret\naz keyvault secret set \\\n  --vault-name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --name \"DatabasePassword\" \\\n  --value \"MySecretPass123\"\n\n# Get a secret\naz keyvault secret show \\\n  --vault-name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --name \"DatabasePassword\" \\\n  --query value --output tsv\n\n# List secrets\naz keyvault secret list --vault-name kv-myapp-dev --output table\n```\n\n### Reference Key Vault from App Service\n\nApp Service can pull secrets from Key Vault using a **Key Vault Reference** — no code changes needed.\n\n```powershell\n# Enable Managed Identity on your App Service\naz webapp identity assign \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning\n\n# Get the managed identity's principal ID\n$principalId = az webapp identity show \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query principalId --output tsv\n\n# Grant the identity access to Key Vault secrets\naz keyvault set-policy \\\n  --name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --object-id $principalId \\\n  --secret-permissions get list\n\n# Now set App Setting as a Key Vault Reference\n# Format: @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=<secret-uri>)\n$secretUri = az keyvault secret show \\\n  --vault-name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --name \"DatabasePassword\" \\\n  --query id --output tsv\n\naz webapp config appsettings set \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --settings \"DATABASE_PASSWORD=@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=$secretUri)\"\n```\n\nYour app reads `DATABASE_PASSWORD` as a normal env variable — Key Vault is transparent.\n\n```viz\nkeyvault-flow\n```\n\n### Key Vault Access Policies vs RBAC\n\nKey Vault supports two authorization models:\n\n- **Access Policies** (legacy, simpler): assign get/list/set per identity\n- **RBAC** (modern, recommended): use role assignments like `Key Vault Secrets User`\n\n```powershell\n# Modern RBAC approach\naz role assignment create \\\n  --role \"Key Vault Secrets User\" \\\n  --assignee $principalId \\\n  --scope /subscriptions/<sub-id>/resourceGroups/rg-learning/providers/Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/kv-myapp-dev\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Azure Virtual Networks\n\nA VNet is a **private network in Azure**. Resources inside a VNet communicate privately without going over the public internet.\n\n### Why VNets?\n\n- Prevent your database from being publicly accessible\n- Allow private communication between app and database\n- Control traffic with Network Security Groups (NSG)\n\n### Core Components\n\n```\nVNet: 10.0.0.0/16\n├── Subnet: 10.0.1.0/24  (app subnet — App Service / Container Apps)\n│   └── NSG (allow 443 inbound, deny everything else)\n└── Subnet: 10.0.2.0/24  (data subnet — PostgreSQL, SQL)\n    └── NSG (allow 5432 from app subnet only)\n```\n\n### Create VNet and Subnets\n\n```powershell\n# Create VNet\naz network vnet create \\\n  --name vnet-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --address-prefix 10.0.0.0/16\n\n# Create app subnet\naz network vnet subnet create \\\n  --name subnet-app \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet-name vnet-myapp \\\n  --address-prefix 10.0.1.0/24\n\n# Create data subnet\naz network vnet subnet create \\\n  --name subnet-data \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet-name vnet-myapp \\\n  --address-prefix 10.0.2.0/24\n```\n\n### Network Security Groups (NSG)\n\nAn NSG is a **firewall** for your subnet or network interface.\n\n```powershell\n# Create NSG\naz network nsg create \\\n  --name nsg-app \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning\n\n# Allow HTTPS inbound\naz network nsg rule create \\\n  --nsg-name nsg-app \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --name AllowHTTPS \\\n  --priority 100 \\\n  --direction Inbound \\\n  --access Allow \\\n  --protocol Tcp \\\n  --destination-port-ranges 443\n\n# Deny all other inbound (Azure auto-creates a DenyAllInbound with priority 65500)\n\n# Associate NSG with subnet\naz network vnet subnet update \\\n  --name subnet-app \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet-name vnet-myapp \\\n  --network-security-group nsg-app\n```\n\n### VNet Integration (App Service)\n\n```powershell\n# Connect App Service to a VNet subnet (outbound traffic goes through VNet)\naz webapp vnet-integration add \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet vnet-myapp \\\n  --subnet subnet-app\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Azure Blob Storage\n\nAzure Blob Storage is **object storage** — store files, images, videos, backups, logs.\n\n### Concepts\n\n```\nStorage Account (globally unique name)\n└── Container (like a bucket or top-level folder)\n    ├── blob1.jpg\n    ├── uploads/avatar.png\n    └── reports/2024/q1.pdf\n```\n\n### Access Tiers\n\n| Tier | Use case | Cost |\n|------|----------|------|\n| Hot | Frequently accessed | Higher storage, lower access cost |\n| Cool | Infrequently accessed (30+ days) | Lower storage, higher access cost |\n| Archive | Rarely accessed (180+ days) | Lowest storage, retrieval takes hours |\n\n### Create Storage Account\n\n```powershell\n# Create storage account\naz storage account create \\\n  --name stmyappdev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus \\\n  --sku Standard_LRS \\\n  --kind StorageV2\n\n# Create a container\naz storage container create \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --name uploads \\\n  --public-access off\n\n# Get connection string\naz storage account show-connection-string \\\n  --name stmyappdev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --output tsv\n```\n\n### Upload / Download Files\n\n```powershell\n# Upload a file\naz storage blob upload \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --container-name uploads \\\n  --name \"images/photo.jpg\" \\\n  --file \"C:\\local\\photo.jpg\"\n\n# List blobs\naz storage blob list \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --container-name uploads \\\n  --output table\n\n# Download a file\naz storage blob download \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --container-name uploads \\\n  --name \"images/photo.jpg\" \\\n  --file \"C:\\downloads\\photo.jpg\"\n\n# Delete a blob\naz storage blob delete \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --container-name uploads \\\n  --name \"images/photo.jpg\"\n```\n\n### Shared Access Signatures (SAS)\n\nA SAS token grants **temporary, scoped access** to a blob without exposing your account key.\n\n```powershell\n# Generate a SAS URL valid for 1 hour\n$expiry = (Get-Date).AddHours(1).ToString(\"yyyy-MM-dTHH:mmZ\")\n\naz storage blob generate-sas \\\n  --account-name stmyappdev \\\n  --container-name uploads \\\n  --name \"images/photo.jpg\" \\\n  --permissions r \\\n  --expiry $expiry \\\n  --output tsv\n# Returns a token — append to the blob URL to allow read access\n```\n\n### Using Blob Storage in Code (C# Example)\n\n```csharp\n// Install: Azure.Storage.Blobs\nusing Azure.Storage.Blobs;\n\nvar client = new BlobServiceClient(connectionString);\nvar container = client.GetBlobContainerClient(\"uploads\");\n\n// Upload\nawait container.UploadBlobAsync(\"images/photo.jpg\", fileStream);\n\n// Download\nvar blob = container.GetBlobClient(\"images/photo.jpg\");\nvar response = await blob.DownloadAsync();\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 15 — Azure Foundations\n```powershell\naz login\naz account list --output table\naz group create --name rg-learning --location eastus\naz group list --output table\n# Explore the Azure Portal at portal.azure.com\n# Find your resource group, browse Cost Management\n```\n\n### Day 16 — Deploy to App Service\n```powershell\n# Create plan and web app\naz appservice plan create --name plan-dev --resource-group rg-learning --sku B1 --is-linux\naz webapp create --name app-<your-name>-dev --resource-group rg-learning --plan plan-dev --deployment-container-image-name nginx\n# Visit the app URL\naz webapp show --name app-<your-name>-dev --resource-group rg-learning --query defaultHostName --output tsv\n# Now deploy YOUR Docker image from ACR\n```\n\n### Day 17 — Deploy to Container Apps\nRedeploy the same image to Container Apps. Compare the experience with App Service. Note: Container Apps scales to zero — after a few minutes idle, the first request will be slow.\n\n### Day 18 — Key Vault\n1. Create a Key Vault\n2. Store your database password as a secret\n3. Enable Managed Identity on your App Service\n4. Grant access and use the Key Vault Reference syntax in app settings\n5. Verify the app reads the secret correctly\n\n### Day 19 — Virtual Network\n1. Create a VNet with two subnets\n2. Create an NSG, add rules\n3. Associate NSG with subnet\n4. Connect App Service to VNet\n\n### Day 20 — Blob Storage\n1. Create a storage account and container\n2. Upload 3 files via CLI\n3. Add file upload to your app that saves to blob storage\n4. Generate a SAS URL for a file and verify it works in browser\n\n### Day 21 — Architecture Review\nDraw your full architecture on paper or in a tool like draw.io. Include:\n- Where your app runs (App Service / Container Apps)\n- Where your secrets live (Key Vault)\n- Where your files go (Blob Storage)\n- Network boundaries (VNet, NSG)\n- Add diagram to your self-review notes\n\n---\n\n## 9. Cheat Sheet\n\n```powershell\n# LOGIN\naz login\naz account show\naz account set --subscription \"Name\"\n\n# RESOURCE GROUPS\naz group create --name NAME --location LOCATION\naz group list --output table\naz group delete --name NAME --yes --no-wait\n\n# APP SERVICE\naz appservice plan create --name NAME --resource-group RG --sku B1 --is-linux\naz webapp create --name NAME --resource-group RG --plan PLAN\naz webapp restart --name NAME --resource-group RG\naz webapp log tail --name NAME --resource-group RG\naz webapp config appsettings set --name NAME --resource-group RG --settings KEY=VALUE\n\n# CONTAINER APPS\naz containerapp create --name NAME --resource-group RG --environment ENV --image IMAGE\naz containerapp update --name NAME --resource-group RG --image NEW_IMAGE\n\n# KEY VAULT\naz keyvault create --name NAME --resource-group RG\naz keyvault secret set --vault-name NAME --name KEY --value VALUE\naz keyvault secret show --vault-name NAME --name KEY --query value -o tsv\n\n# STORAGE\naz storage account create --name NAME --resource-group RG --sku Standard_LRS\naz storage container create --account-name ACCT --name CONTAINER\naz storage blob upload --account-name ACCT --container-name C --name BLOB --file FILE\n```\n","03-azure-email":"# Phase 2 — Azure Email (Azure Communication Services)\n\n> **Duration:** Days 22–24 | **Goal:** Send transactional emails from your app using Azure\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [What is Azure Communication Services?](#1-what-is-azure-communication-services)\n2. [Setup ACS Email](#2-setup-acs-email)\n3. [Verify a Sender Domain](#3-verify-a-sender-domain)\n4. [Send Email via SDK](#4-send-email-via-sdk)\n5. [Email Templates and Scenarios](#5-email-templates-and-scenarios)\n6. [Error Handling and Retry](#6-error-handling-and-retry)\n7. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#7-day-by-day-exercises)\n\n---\n\n## 1. What is Azure Communication Services?\n\nAzure Communication Services (ACS) is a managed platform for:\n- **Email** — transactional and marketing emails\n- **SMS** — text messages\n- **Voice/Video** — calling and video conferencing\n- **Chat** — real-time messaging\n\nFor most backend apps, you'll use ACS for **transactional email** (welcome emails, password resets, notifications).\n\n### ACS vs SendGrid vs SMTP\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons |\n|--------|------|------|\n| ACS Email | Native Azure, free tier (10k/month), no vendor lock-in | Newer service, less ecosystem |\n| SendGrid | Industry standard, mature SDK | 3rd party, separate account |\n| SMTP relay | Simple | Not scalable, delivery issues |\n\n### Free Tier\n\n- **100 emails/day** free\n- **10,000 emails/month** free\n- Paid: ~$0.0003/email after free tier\n\n---\n\n## 2. Setup ACS Email\n\n### Create ACS Resource\n\n```powershell\n# Register provider if needed\naz provider register --namespace Microsoft.Communication\n\n# Create ACS resource\naz communication create \\\n  --name acs-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location global \\\n  --data-location unitedstates\n\n# Create an Email Communication Service resource (separate from ACS)\naz communication email create \\\n  --name email-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location global \\\n  --data-location unitedstates\n```\n\n> **Note:** ACS has two pieces: the main Communication resource (for SMS, voice) and a separate Email Communication Service. You need both for email.\n\n### Link Email to ACS\n\nIn the Azure Portal:\n1. Go to your ACS resource\n2. Click **Email** > **Connect Email Domain**\n3. Link it to your Email Communication Service\n\nOr via CLI:\n```powershell\naz communication link-notification-hub \\\n  --name acs-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning\n```\n\n### Get Connection String\n\n```powershell\naz communication list-key \\\n  --name acs-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query primaryConnectionString \\\n  --output tsv\n```\n\nStore this in Key Vault:\n```powershell\naz keyvault secret set \\\n  --vault-name kv-myapp-dev \\\n  --name \"AcsConnectionString\" \\\n  --value \"<your-connection-string>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verify a Sender Domain\n\nYou need a verified sender domain or email address before sending.\n\n### Option A — Azure-Managed Domain (Easiest for Dev)\n\nACS provides a free `@azurecomm.net` domain. No DNS setup needed.\n\nIn Azure Portal:\n1. Go to your **Email Communication Service**\n2. Click **Domains** > **Add Domain**\n3. Select **Azure Managed Domain**\n4. Note the address: `DoNotReply@<random>.azurecomm.net`\n\n### Option B — Custom Domain (Production)\n\n1. Go to **Email Communication Service** > **Domains** > **Add Domain**\n2. Select **Custom Domain**\n3. Enter your domain (e.g., `myapp.com`)\n4. Add these DNS records to your domain registrar:\n\n```\nTXT record:  @ / _dmarc.myapp.com   (DMARC/SPF verification)\nCNAME record:                        (DKIM signing)\n```\n\n5. Click **Verify** after DNS propagates (can take up to 48h)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Send Email via SDK\n\n### Install SDK\n\n```powershell\n# .NET\ndotnet add package Azure.Communication.Email\n\n# Node.js\nnpm install @azure-rest/communication-email\n\n# Python\npip install azure-communication-email\n```\n\n### C# (.NET)\n\n```csharp\nusing Azure;\nusing Azure.Communication.Email;\n\npublic class EmailService\n{\n    private readonly EmailClient _client;\n    private readonly string _senderAddress;\n\n    public EmailService(string connectionString, string senderAddress)\n    {\n        _client = new EmailClient(connectionString);\n        _senderAddress = senderAddress;\n    }\n\n    public async Task SendAsync(string toEmail, string toName, string subject, string htmlBody)\n    {\n        var message = new EmailMessage(\n            senderAddress: _senderAddress,\n            recipients: new EmailRecipients(new List<EmailAddress>\n            {\n                new EmailAddress(toEmail, toName)\n            }),\n            content: new EmailContent(subject)\n            {\n                Html = htmlBody,\n                PlainText = StripHtml(htmlBody) // always provide plain text fallback\n            }\n        );\n\n        var operation = await _client.SendAsync(WaitUntil.Completed, message);\n\n        if (operation.Value.Status == EmailSendStatus.Succeeded)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"Email sent: {operation.Id}\");\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            throw new Exception($\"Email failed: {operation.Value.Status}\");\n        }\n    }\n\n    private string StripHtml(string html)\n    {\n        return System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Replace(html, \"<.*?>\", string.Empty);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Register in ASP.NET Core DI\n\n```csharp\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton(sp =>\n{\n    var connectionString = builder.Configuration[\"AcsConnectionString\"];\n    var senderAddress = builder.Configuration[\"AcsSenderAddress\"];\n    return new EmailService(connectionString!, senderAddress!);\n});\n```\n\n### Node.js (TypeScript)\n\n```typescript\nimport EmailClient, { KnownEmailSendStatus } from \"@azure-rest/communication-email\";\n\nconst client = EmailClient(process.env.ACS_CONNECTION_STRING!);\n\nexport async function sendEmail(\n  to: string,\n  subject: string,\n  html: string\n): Promise<void> {\n  const message = {\n    senderAddress: process.env.ACS_SENDER_ADDRESS!,\n    content: {\n      subject,\n      html,\n      plainText: html.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, \"\"),\n    },\n    recipients: {\n      to: [{ address: to }],\n    },\n  };\n\n  const poller = await client.path(\"/emails:send\").post({ body: message });\n  const response = await poller.pollUntilDone();\n\n  if (response.status !== KnownEmailSendStatus.Succeeded) {\n    throw new Error(`Email send failed: ${response.status}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Python\n\n```python\nfrom azure.communication.email import EmailClient\nimport os\n\nclient = EmailClient.from_connection_string(os.environ[\"ACS_CONNECTION_STRING\"])\n\ndef send_email(to_email: str, subject: str, html_body: str) -> None:\n    message = {\n        \"senderAddress\": os.environ[\"ACS_SENDER_ADDRESS\"],\n        \"recipients\": {\n            \"to\": [{\"address\": to_email}]\n        },\n        \"content\": {\n            \"subject\": subject,\n            \"html\": html_body,\n            \"plainText\": html_body  # simplified — strip tags in production\n        }\n    }\n\n    poller = client.begin_send(message)\n    result = poller.result()\n\n    if result[\"status\"] != \"Succeeded\":\n        raise Exception(f\"Email failed: {result['status']}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Email Templates and Scenarios\n\n### Welcome Email\n\n```csharp\npublic string BuildWelcomeEmail(string userName)\n{\n    return $\"\"\"\n        <!DOCTYPE html>\n        <html>\n        <body style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n            <h1>Welcome to MyApp, {userName}!</h1>\n            <p>Your account has been created successfully.</p>\n            <p>\n                <a href=\"https://myapp.com/dashboard\"\n                   style=\"background: #0078D4; color: white; padding: 12px 24px;\n                          text-decoration: none; border-radius: 4px;\">\n                    Go to Dashboard\n                </a>\n            </p>\n            <p>If you have any questions, reply to this email.</p>\n            <hr/>\n            <small>MyApp Inc. | Unsubscribe</small>\n        </body>\n        </html>\n        \"\"\";\n}\n```\n\n### Password Reset Email\n\n```csharp\npublic string BuildPasswordResetEmail(string userName, string resetUrl)\n{\n    return $\"\"\"\n        <!DOCTYPE html>\n        <html>\n        <body style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n            <h2>Password Reset Request</h2>\n            <p>Hi {userName},</p>\n            <p>We received a request to reset your password. Click the button below.\n               This link expires in <strong>1 hour</strong>.</p>\n            <p>\n                <a href=\"{resetUrl}\"\n                   style=\"background: #D83B01; color: white; padding: 12px 24px;\n                          text-decoration: none; border-radius: 4px;\">\n                    Reset Password\n                </a>\n            </p>\n            <p>If you didn't request this, you can safely ignore this email.</p>\n        </body>\n        </html>\n        \"\"\";\n}\n```\n\n### Email with Attachment\n\n```csharp\nvar message = new EmailMessage(senderAddress, recipients, content);\n\n// Add attachment\nvar fileBytes = await File.ReadAllBytesAsync(\"report.pdf\");\nmessage.Attachments.Add(new EmailAttachment(\n    name: \"report.pdf\",\n    mediaType: \"application/pdf\",\n    contentInBase64: BinaryData.FromBytes(fileBytes)\n));\n```\n\n### Multiple Recipients\n\n```csharp\nvar recipients = new EmailRecipients(\n    to: new[] { new EmailAddress(\"user@example.com\", \"User\") },\n    cc: new[] { new EmailAddress(\"manager@example.com\", \"Manager\") },\n    bcc: new[] { new EmailAddress(\"audit@example.com\") }\n);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Error Handling and Retry\n\n### ACS Email Status Flow\n\n```viz\nemail-status\n```\n\n```\nRequest submitted\n    ↓\nQueued\n    ↓\nOutForDelivery\n    ↓\nSucceeded ─── or ─── Failed (bounce, spam, etc.)\n```\n\n### Polling for Status (C#)\n\n```csharp\nvar operation = await _client.SendAsync(WaitUntil.Started, message);\n\n// Poll manually with timeout\nvar timeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2);\nvar deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;\n\nwhile (!operation.HasCompleted && DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)\n{\n    await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));\n    await operation.UpdateStatusAsync();\n}\n\nvar status = operation.Value.Status;\nswitch (status)\n{\n    case EmailSendStatus.Succeeded:\n        // log success\n        break;\n    case EmailSendStatus.Failed:\n        // log failure, consider retry or alert\n        throw new Exception($\"Email delivery failed for {toEmail}\");\n    default:\n        // still in progress — save operation.Id and check later\n        break;\n}\n```\n\n### Best Practices\n\n1. **Idempotency** — generate a unique `operationId` per email so retries don't send duplicates\n2. **Dead letter queue** — if email fails after 3 retries, write to a queue for manual review\n3. **Rate limiting** — ACS has limits; don't send bulk emails synchronously\n4. **Logging** — always log the operation ID so you can track delivery in Azure Portal\n5. **Unsubscribe** — always include an unsubscribe link for marketing emails (legal requirement)\n\n---\n\n## 7. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 22 — Setup ACS\n1. Create an ACS resource and Email Communication Service via CLI or Portal\n2. Set up an Azure Managed Domain (free, no DNS needed)\n3. Get the connection string and store it in Key Vault\n4. In the Portal, use the **Test Send** feature to send a test email to yourself\n\n### Day 23 — Send from Code\n1. Install the ACS Email SDK for your language\n2. Write a simple function that sends an email\n3. Call it from an API endpoint (e.g., `POST /test-email`)\n4. Verify delivery — check spam folder if not in inbox\n\n### Day 24 — Templates + Scenarios\n1. Build a welcome email template with HTML\n2. Build a password reset template with a time-limited link\n3. Add error handling with retries\n4. Test with an invalid email address and observe the error\n5. Add email sending to your mini-project's user registration flow\n","04-azure-authentication":"# Phase 2 — Azure Authentication (Microsoft Entra ID)\n\n> **Duration:** Days 25–28 | **Goal:** Add \"Login with Microsoft\" and RBAC to your app\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Identity Concepts](#1-identity-concepts)\n2. [Microsoft Entra ID Fundamentals](#2-microsoft-entra-id-fundamentals)\n3. [App Registration](#3-app-registration)\n4. [OAuth 2.0 and OIDC Flows](#4-oauth-20-and-oidc-flows)\n5. [Implement Authentication](#5-implement-authentication)\n6. [Role-Based Access Control](#6-role-based-access-control)\n7. [Calling Protected APIs](#7-calling-protected-apis)\n8. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#8-day-by-day-exercises)\n9. [Cheat Sheet](#9-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Identity Concepts\n\n### Authentication vs Authorization\n\n```\nAuthentication (AuthN): Who are you? → Verify identity (login)\nAuthorization  (AuthZ): What can you do? → Check permissions\n```\n\n### Key Terms\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| **Identity Provider (IdP)** | The system that manages user identities (Entra ID, Google, Auth0) |\n| **Token** | A signed piece of data proving identity and claims |\n| **Access Token** | Short-lived token to call APIs (15 min – 1 hour) |\n| **Refresh Token** | Long-lived token to get new access tokens |\n| **ID Token** | Token with user info (name, email) for the app |\n| **Claim** | A key-value pair inside a token (e.g., `email: user@company.com`) |\n| **Scope** | Permission the app is requesting (e.g., `User.Read`) |\n| **Tenant** | An organization's instance of Entra ID |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Microsoft Entra ID Fundamentals\n\nMicrosoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) is Microsoft's **cloud identity platform**. Every Azure subscription has a tenant.\n\n### Tenant Types\n\n| Type | When to use |\n|------|------------|\n| **Single-tenant** | App is only for your organization's users |\n| **Multi-tenant** | App is a SaaS — any Microsoft org can sign in |\n| **Personal accounts** | Allow @outlook.com, @hotmail.com accounts |\n\n### Key Objects in Entra ID\n\n```\nTenant (your org's directory)\n├── Users (people who can sign in)\n├── Groups (collections of users)\n├── App Registrations (your app's identity)\n│   └── Service Principal (the running instance of the app)\n├── Enterprise Applications (3rd party apps your users can use)\n└── Roles (permissions you can assign to users/groups)\n```\n\n### Find Your Tenant ID\n\n```powershell\naz account show --query tenantId --output tsv\n```\n\nOr in Azure Portal: **Microsoft Entra ID** > **Overview** > **Tenant ID**\n\n---\n\n## 3. App Registration\n\nAn **App Registration** is how you register your application with Entra ID. It's what allows your app to request tokens.\n\n### Create App Registration\n\n#### Via Portal (recommended for learning)\n\n1. Go to **Microsoft Entra ID** > **App registrations** > **New registration**\n2. Enter a name: `myapp-dev`\n3. Choose **Supported account types**:\n   - *Single tenant* — only your org\n   - *Multi-tenant* — any Azure AD org\n   - *Multi-tenant + personal* — anyone\n4. Set **Redirect URI**: `http://localhost:3000/auth/callback` for dev\n5. Click **Register**\n\nSave these values:\n- **Application (client) ID** — your app's unique ID\n- **Directory (tenant) ID** — your tenant\n- **Client Secret** (create under Certificates & Secrets) — your app's password\n\n#### Via CLI\n\n```powershell\n# Create app registration\naz ad app create \\\n  --display-name \"myapp-dev\" \\\n  --sign-in-audience AzureADMyOrg \\\n  --web-redirect-uris \"http://localhost:3000/auth/callback\"\n\n# Get the app ID\n$appId = az ad app list --display-name \"myapp-dev\" --query \"[0].appId\" --output tsv\n\n# Create a client secret\naz ad app credential reset \\\n  --id $appId \\\n  --display-name \"dev-secret\"\n# Copy the password — it's only shown once!\n```\n\n### App Registration vs Service Principal\n\n```\nApp Registration  = the definition (like a class)\nService Principal = the instance in a specific tenant (like an object)\n\nWhen you register an app, a Service Principal is auto-created in your tenant.\nWhen a user from another tenant installs your multi-tenant app,\na Service Principal is created in THEIR tenant.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. OAuth 2.0 and OIDC Flows\n\n### Authorization Code Flow (Web Apps)\n\nThis is the correct flow for web apps where a user signs in:\n\n```viz\noauth-flow\n```\n\n```\nUser clicks \"Sign in\"\n    ↓\nApp redirects to Entra ID:\nhttps://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/oauth2/v2.0/authorize\n  ?client_id=<app-id>\n  &response_type=code\n  &redirect_uri=<your-callback>\n  &scope=openid profile email\n  &state=<random-state>\n    ↓\nUser logs in at Microsoft's login page\n    ↓\nMicrosoft redirects back to your app with an authorization CODE\nGET /auth/callback?code=<auth-code>&state=<state>\n    ↓\nYour app exchanges the CODE for tokens (server-side, secure):\nPOST https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/oauth2/v2.0/token\n  client_id, client_secret, code, redirect_uri, grant_type=authorization_code\n    ↓\nMicrosoft returns:\n  - access_token (call APIs)\n  - id_token (user info)\n  - refresh_token (get new tokens)\n    ↓\nApp stores tokens, user is logged in\n```\n\n### Client Credentials Flow (Service-to-Service)\n\nNo user involved — your service authenticates as itself:\n\n```\nYour Service\n    ↓\nPOST https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/oauth2/v2.0/token\n  client_id=<app-id>\n  client_secret=<secret>\n  scope=<resource>/.default\n  grant_type=client_credentials\n    ↓\nMicrosoft returns access_token\n    ↓\nUse token to call another API\n```\n\nUse this for: background jobs, microservice-to-microservice calls, CI/CD pipelines.\n\n```viz\ntoken-types\n```\n\n### Token Contents\n\nDecode any JWT at [jwt.ms](https://jwt.ms). A typical ID token has:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"oid\": \"user-object-id-in-azure-ad\",\n  \"sub\": \"unique-user-identifier\",\n  \"name\": \"John Smith\",\n  \"email\": \"john@company.com\",\n  \"preferred_username\": \"john@company.com\",\n  \"tid\": \"tenant-id\",\n  \"aud\": \"your-app-client-id\",\n  \"exp\": 1700000000,\n  \"roles\": [\"Admin\", \"User\"]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implement Authentication\n\n### ASP.NET Core — Microsoft Identity Web\n\n```powershell\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Identity.Web\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Identity.Web.UI\n```\n\n```json\n// appsettings.json\n{\n  \"AzureAd\": {\n    \"Instance\": \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/\",\n    \"TenantId\": \"<your-tenant-id>\",\n    \"ClientId\": \"<your-app-client-id>\",\n    \"ClientSecret\": \"<your-client-secret>\",\n    \"CallbackPath\": \"/signin-oidc\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n```csharp\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services\n    .AddAuthentication(OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)\n    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApp(builder.Configuration.GetSection(\"AzureAd\"));\n\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization();\n\n// ...\napp.UseAuthentication();\napp.UseAuthorization();\n```\n\n```csharp\n// Protect a controller or endpoint\n[Authorize]\npublic class DashboardController : Controller\n{\n    public IActionResult Index()\n    {\n        var userName = User.FindFirst(\"name\")?.Value;\n        var userEmail = User.FindFirst(\"preferred_username\")?.Value;\n        return View();\n    }\n}\n\n// Or protect a minimal API endpoint\napp.MapGet(\"/api/profile\", (ClaimsPrincipal user) =>\n{\n    return new { Name = user.FindFirst(\"name\")?.Value };\n}).RequireAuthorization();\n```\n\n### Node.js — MSAL Node\n\n```powershell\nnpm install @azure/msal-node express-session\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport { ConfidentialClientApplication } from \"@azure/msal-node\";\nimport express from \"express\";\nimport session from \"express-session\";\n\nconst msalConfig = {\n  auth: {\n    clientId: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_ID!,\n    authority: `https://login.microsoftonline.com/${process.env.AZURE_TENANT_ID}`,\n    clientSecret: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET!,\n  },\n};\n\nconst pca = new ConfidentialClientApplication(msalConfig);\nconst app = express();\n\napp.use(session({ secret: \"your-secret\", resave: false, saveUninitialized: false }));\n\n// Redirect to Microsoft login\napp.get(\"/auth/login\", async (req, res) => {\n  const authUrl = await pca.getAuthCodeUrl({\n    scopes: [\"openid\", \"profile\", \"email\"],\n    redirectUri: \"http://localhost:3000/auth/callback\",\n  });\n  res.redirect(authUrl);\n});\n\n// Handle callback\napp.get(\"/auth/callback\", async (req, res) => {\n  const result = await pca.acquireTokenByCode({\n    code: req.query.code as string,\n    scopes: [\"openid\", \"profile\", \"email\"],\n    redirectUri: \"http://localhost:3000/auth/callback\",\n  });\n\n  (req.session as any).user = result.account;\n  res.redirect(\"/\");\n});\n\n// Middleware to check authentication\nfunction requireAuth(req: express.Request, res: express.Response, next: express.NextFunction) {\n  if (!(req.session as any).user) {\n    return res.redirect(\"/auth/login\");\n  }\n  next();\n}\n\napp.get(\"/dashboard\", requireAuth, (req, res) => {\n  res.json({ user: (req.session as any).user });\n});\n```\n\n### Protecting an API (Validate Bearer Tokens)\n\nWhen your frontend calls your API with an access token:\n\n```csharp\n// ASP.NET Core API — validate JWT tokens from Entra ID\nbuilder.Services\n    .AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)\n    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection(\"AzureAd\"));\n\n// Endpoint\n[Authorize]\n[HttpGet(\"api/data\")]\npublic IActionResult GetData()\n{\n    var userId = User.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value;\n    return Ok(new { UserId = userId });\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Role-Based Access Control\n\n### App Roles (Custom Roles for Your App)\n\nApp roles let you define permissions specific to your application.\n\n#### Define Roles in App Registration\n\nIn Azure Portal:\n1. Go to your App Registration > **App roles**\n2. Click **Create app role**\n3. Fill in:\n   - Display name: `Admin`\n   - Allowed member types: `Users/Groups`\n   - Value: `app.admin` ← this appears in the token\n   - Description: `Full access to admin panel`\n4. Repeat for other roles: `Viewer`, `Editor`\n\n#### Assign Roles to Users\n\n1. Go to **Enterprise Applications** > find your app\n2. Click **Users and groups** > **Add user/group**\n3. Select a user and assign a role\n\n#### Check Roles in Code\n\n```csharp\n// ASP.NET Core — require a specific role\n[Authorize(Roles = \"app.admin\")]\npublic IActionResult AdminPanel()\n{\n    return View();\n}\n\n// Or check programmatically\nif (User.IsInRole(\"app.admin\"))\n{\n    // show admin features\n}\n\n// Or via policy\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>\n{\n    options.AddPolicy(\"AdminOnly\", policy =>\n        policy.RequireRole(\"app.admin\"));\n    options.AddPolicy(\"ReadWrite\", policy =>\n        policy.RequireRole(\"app.admin\", \"app.editor\"));\n});\n\n[Authorize(Policy = \"AdminOnly\")]\npublic IActionResult DeleteUser(int id) { ... }\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Node.js — check roles from decoded token\nfunction requireRole(role: string) {\n  return (req: express.Request, res: express.Response, next: express.NextFunction) => {\n    const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(\" \")[1];\n    const decoded = jwt.decode(token!) as any;\n\n    if (!decoded?.roles?.includes(role)) {\n      return res.status(403).json({ error: \"Forbidden\" });\n    }\n    next();\n  };\n}\n\napp.delete(\"/api/users/:id\", requireAuth, requireRole(\"app.admin\"), (req, res) => {\n  // only admins can delete users\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Calling Protected APIs\n\n### From a Web App to Microsoft Graph\n\nMicrosoft Graph lets you read/write user data (profile, calendar, email, etc.).\n\n```csharp\n// Install\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Identity.Web.MicrosoftGraph\n\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services\n    .AddAuthentication(...)\n    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApp(builder.Configuration.GetSection(\"AzureAd\"))\n    .EnableTokenAcquisitionToCallDownstreamApi(new[] { \"User.Read\" })\n    .AddMicrosoftGraph(builder.Configuration.GetSection(\"MicrosoftGraph\"))\n    .AddInMemoryTokenCaches();\n\n// Controller\npublic class ProfileController : Controller\n{\n    private readonly GraphServiceClient _graphClient;\n\n    public ProfileController(GraphServiceClient graphClient)\n    {\n        _graphClient = graphClient;\n    }\n\n    [Authorize]\n    public async Task<IActionResult> Index()\n    {\n        var user = await _graphClient.Me.GetAsync();\n        return View(user);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Service-to-Service (Client Credentials)\n\n```csharp\n// Get a token for another API (no user involved)\nvar credential = new ClientSecretCredential(tenantId, clientId, clientSecret);\nvar tokenRequestContext = new TokenRequestContext(new[] { \"api://<target-app-id>/.default\" });\nvar token = await credential.GetTokenAsync(tokenRequestContext);\n\nvar httpClient = new HttpClient();\nhttpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization =\n    new AuthenticationHeaderValue(\"Bearer\", token.Token);\n\nvar response = await httpClient.GetAsync(\"https://other-api.azurewebsites.net/api/data\");\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 25 — Entra ID Basics\n1. Go to **Microsoft Entra ID** in Azure Portal\n2. Browse: Users, Groups, App Registrations, Enterprise Applications\n3. Create an App Registration for `myapp-dev`\n4. Note: Client ID, Tenant ID\n5. Create a Client Secret and save it to Key Vault\n6. Decode a token at [jwt.ms](https://jwt.ms) — understand the claims\n\n### Day 26 — Login with Microsoft\n1. Add MSAL or Microsoft.Identity.Web to your app\n2. Implement the authorization code flow\n3. Add a `/login` and `/logout` route\n4. Display the logged-in user's name on the home page\n5. Protect one route so it redirects to login\n\n### Day 27 — RBAC\n1. Define two app roles: `app.admin` and `app.viewer`\n2. Assign yourself the `app.admin` role\n3. Protect an admin endpoint with `[Authorize(Roles = \"app.admin\")]`\n4. Verify: admin role can access it, no role gets 403\n\n### Day 28 — Review\n1. Self-demo: walk through the login flow end to end\n2. Show role enforcement working\n3. Discuss: how would you implement refresh token rotation?\n\n---\n\n## 9. Cheat Sheet\n\n```\nTenant ID:      az account show --query tenantId -o tsv\nApp Client ID:  az ad app list --display-name \"myapp-dev\" --query \"[0].appId\" -o tsv\n\nAuthorization endpoint:\nhttps://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/oauth2/v2.0/authorize\n\nToken endpoint:\nhttps://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/oauth2/v2.0/token\n\nJWKS (for manual token validation):\nhttps://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant}/discovery/v2.0/keys\n\nDecode tokens:   https://jwt.ms\nMSAL docs:       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/active-directory/develop/\nGraph Explorer:  https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/graph-explorer\n```\n","05-deploy-database":"# Phase 3 — Deploy Database\n\n> **Duration:** Days 29–35 (Week 5) | **Goal:** Provision, connect, secure, and manage a production-grade Azure database\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Choose the Right Database](#1-choose-the-right-database)\n2. [Azure SQL Database](#2-azure-sql-database)\n3. [Azure Database for PostgreSQL](#3-azure-database-for-postgresql)\n4. [Managed Identity Authentication](#4-managed-identity-authentication)\n5. [Database Migrations](#5-database-migrations)\n6. [Backups and Restore](#6-backups-and-restore)\n7. [Monitoring and Performance](#7-monitoring-and-performance)\n8. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#8-day-by-day-exercises)\n9. [Cheat Sheet](#9-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Choose the Right Database\n\n### Decision Matrix\n\n| | Azure SQL | PostgreSQL Flexible | Cosmos DB |\n|--|-----------|---------------------|-----------|\n| **Type** | Relational (T-SQL) | Relational (ANSI SQL) | NoSQL (document, key-value, graph) |\n| **Best for** | .NET apps, existing SQL Server | Node/Python apps, open-source workloads | Global distribution, variable schema |\n| **Minimum cost** | ~$5/month (serverless) | ~$12/month (Burstable B1ms) | ~$0 (free tier: 1000 RU/s, 25GB) |\n| **Managed features** | High availability, backups, patching | High availability, backups, patching | Global replication, multi-write |\n| **Extensions/languages** | T-SQL only | Full PostgreSQL extensions | N/A |\n\n**Recommendation for learning:** Use **PostgreSQL Flexible Server** if you prefer open-source SQL, or **Azure SQL** if you're .NET-focused.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Azure SQL Database\n\n### Concepts\n\n```\nSQL Server (logical server — manages logins, firewall)\n└── SQL Database (the actual database — you're billed here)\n\nYou can have many databases under one server.\nServer has a globally unique name: <server>.database.windows.net\n```\n\n### Purchasing Models\n\n- **DTU model** — simple, bundled compute+storage (Basic: 5 DTU, ~$5/mo)\n- **vCore model** — more control, Serverless option (pauses when idle, minimum billing)\n- **Serverless** — great for dev: auto-pause after inactivity, pay per second of use\n\n### Provision Azure SQL\n\n```powershell\n# Create SQL Server\naz sql server create \\\n  --name sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus \\\n  --admin-user sqladmin \\\n  --admin-password \"$(az keyvault secret show --vault-name kv-myapp-dev --name SqlAdminPassword --query value -o tsv)\"\n\n# Create Database (Serverless — ideal for dev)\naz sql db create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name myappdb \\\n  --edition GeneralPurpose \\\n  --family Gen5 \\\n  --capacity 2 \\\n  --compute-model Serverless \\\n  --auto-pause-delay 60\n\n# Get connection string\naz sql db show-connection-string \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name myappdb \\\n  --client ado.net\n```\n\n### Firewall Rules\n\nBy default, Azure SQL blocks all traffic. You must explicitly allow:\n\n```powershell\n# Allow Azure services (App Service, Container Apps)\naz sql server firewall-rule create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name AllowAzureServices \\\n  --start-ip-address 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --end-ip-address 0.0.0.0\n\n# Allow your local dev machine\n$myIp = (Invoke-RestMethod https://api.ipify.org)\naz sql server firewall-rule create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name AllowMyDev \\\n  --start-ip-address $myIp \\\n  --end-ip-address $myIp\n```\n\n### Connect from Local\n\n```powershell\n# Using sqlcmd\nsqlcmd -S sql-myapp-dev.database.windows.net -d myappdb -U sqladmin -P <password>\n\n# Using Azure Data Studio or SSMS\n# Server: sql-myapp-dev.database.windows.net\n# Authentication: SQL Login\n# Username: sqladmin\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Azure Database for PostgreSQL\n\n### Flexible Server (Recommended)\n\n```\nFlexible Server = full control over the VM, private VNet support, zones\nSingle Server   = legacy, being retired\n```\n\n### Provision PostgreSQL Flexible Server\n\n```powershell\n# Create PostgreSQL Flexible Server\naz postgres flexible-server create \\\n  --name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus \\\n  --admin-user pgadmin \\\n  --admin-password \"YourStr0ngPassword!\" \\\n  --sku-name Standard_B1ms \\\n  --tier Burstable \\\n  --version 16 \\\n  --storage-size 32 \\\n  --public-access 0.0.0.0\n\n# This also creates a default database \"flexibleserverdb\"\n# Create your own database\naz postgres flexible-server db create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server-name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --database-name myappdb\n\n# Get connection info\naz postgres flexible-server show \\\n  --name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query \"{Host:fullyQualifiedDomainName}\" \\\n  --output table\n```\n\n### Firewall Rules\n\n```powershell\n# Allow Azure services\naz postgres flexible-server firewall-rule create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --rule-name AllowAzure \\\n  --start-ip-address 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --end-ip-address 0.0.0.0\n\n# Allow your local IP\n$myIp = (Invoke-RestMethod https://api.ipify.org)\naz postgres flexible-server firewall-rule create \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --rule-name AllowDev \\\n  --start-ip-address $myIp \\\n  --end-ip-address $myIp\n```\n\n### Connect from Local\n\n```powershell\n# Using psql (install with PostgreSQL client)\npsql \"host=pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com dbname=myappdb user=pgadmin password=<pass> sslmode=require\"\n\n# Connection string format\n# PostgreSQL: postgresql://pgadmin:pass@pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com/myappdb?sslmode=require\n# .NET: Host=pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com;Database=myappdb;Username=pgadmin;Password=pass;SSL Mode=Require\n```\n\n### VNet Integration (Production Best Practice)\n\n```powershell\n# Create a subnet with service delegation for PostgreSQL\naz network vnet subnet create \\\n  --name subnet-postgres \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet-name vnet-myapp \\\n  --address-prefix 10.0.3.0/24 \\\n  --delegations Microsoft.DBforPostgreSQL/flexibleServers\n\n# Create server inside VNet (no public access)\naz postgres flexible-server create \\\n  --name pg-myapp-prod \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --vnet vnet-myapp \\\n  --subnet subnet-postgres \\\n  --private-dns-zone myapp.private.postgres.database.azure.com \\\n  --public-access None \\\n  # ... other params\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Managed Identity Authentication\n\nManaged Identity eliminates database passwords entirely. Azure automatically handles token exchange.\n\n```viz\nmanaged-identity\n```\n\n### How It Works\n\n```\nYour App (App Service / Container App)\n    |\n    | \"Give me a token for PostgreSQL\"\n    v\nAzure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) — local token endpoint\n    |\n    | returns short-lived access token\n    v\nYour App connects to PostgreSQL using the token instead of a password\n    |\nPostgreSQL validates token against Entra ID\n```\n\n### Setup — PostgreSQL + Managed Identity\n\n```powershell\n# 1. Enable Managed Identity on your App Service\naz webapp identity assign \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning\n\n# Get the identity's object ID\n$objectId = az webapp identity show \\\n  --name app-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query principalId \\\n  --output tsv\n\n# 2. Enable Entra ID auth on PostgreSQL\naz postgres flexible-server ad-admin set \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server-name pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --display-name \"app-myapp-dev\" \\\n  --object-id $objectId\n\n# 3. Create the user in PostgreSQL (connect as admin first)\n# psql command:\n# CREATE ROLE \"app-myapp-dev\" WITH LOGIN;\n# GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE myappdb TO \"app-myapp-dev\";\n# GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO \"app-myapp-dev\";\n# GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"app-myapp-dev\";\n```\n\n### Connect with Managed Identity (C#)\n\n```csharp\n// Install Npgsql with Azure support\ndotnet add package Npgsql\ndotnet add package Azure.Identity\n\nusing Azure.Identity;\nusing Npgsql;\n\nvar credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();\n\n// Get PostgreSQL token\nvar tokenRequestContext = new TokenRequestContext(\n    new[] { \"https://ossrdbms-aad.database.windows.net/.default\" }\n);\nvar token = await credential.GetTokenAsync(tokenRequestContext);\n\nvar connectionString = $\"Host=pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com;\" +\n                       $\"Database=myappdb;\" +\n                       $\"Username=app-myapp-dev;\" +\n                       $\"SSL Mode=Require;\";\n\nawait using var conn = new NpgsqlConnection(connectionString);\nconn.ProvidePasswordCallback = (host, port, db, user) => token.Token;\nawait conn.OpenAsync();\n```\n\n### Connect with Managed Identity (Azure SQL)\n\n```csharp\n// EF Core — Azure SQL with Managed Identity\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Data.SqlClient\ndotnet add package Azure.Identity\n\n// Connection string — no password needed\n// \"Server=sql-myapp-dev.database.windows.net;Database=myappdb;Authentication=Active Directory Default;\"\n\n// Or programmatically\nusing Azure.Identity;\nusing Microsoft.Data.SqlClient;\n\nvar credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();\nvar tokenRequestContext = new TokenRequestContext(\n    new[] { \"https://database.windows.net/.default\" }\n);\nvar token = await credential.GetTokenAsync(tokenRequestContext);\n\nvar conn = new SqlConnection(\"Server=sql-myapp-dev.database.windows.net;Database=myappdb;\");\nconn.AccessToken = token.Token;\nawait conn.OpenAsync();\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database Migrations\n\nNever manually alter a production database schema. Use a migration tool so schema changes are version-controlled and repeatable.\n\n### EF Core Migrations (.NET)\n\n```powershell\n# Install EF Core tools\ndotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef\ndotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design\n\n# Create a migration\ndotnet ef migrations add CreateUsersTable\n\n# Apply migrations to database\ndotnet ef database update\n\n# Generate SQL script (for review before applying to prod)\ndotnet ef migrations script --idempotent -o migrations.sql\n\n# Rollback one migration\ndotnet ef database update PreviousMigrationName\n```\n\n```csharp\n// Apply migrations on app startup (for containers/CI/CD)\n// Program.cs\nusing var scope = app.Services.CreateScope();\nvar db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<AppDbContext>();\nawait db.Database.MigrateAsync();\n```\n\n### Flyway (Any Language)\n\nFlyway manages migrations as numbered SQL files.\n\n```\ndb/migration/\n├── V1__Create_users_table.sql\n├── V2__Add_email_index.sql\n├── V3__Create_orders_table.sql\n```\n\n```sql\n-- V1__Create_users_table.sql\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n```\n\n```powershell\n# Run migrations (Docker-based)\ndocker run --rm \\\n  -v \"${PWD}/db/migration:/flyway/sql\" \\\n  flyway/flyway:10 \\\n  -url=\"jdbc:postgresql://pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com/myappdb\" \\\n  -user=pgadmin \\\n  -password=<pass> \\\n  migrate\n\n# Check migration status\ndocker run --rm flyway/flyway:10 ... info\n```\n\n### Migration Best Practices\n\n1. **Never edit an applied migration** — create a new one instead\n2. **Always test rollback** — write a corresponding undo migration\n3. **Idempotent scripts** — use `IF NOT EXISTS`, `IF EXISTS` guards\n4. **Separate data migrations** — schema and data changes in different migrations\n5. **Run migrations before deploying new code** — never after\n6. **Keep migrations small** — one concern per migration\n\n---\n\n## 6. Backups and Restore\n\n### Automated Backups\n\nAzure SQL and PostgreSQL both do automated backups automatically:\n\n| | Azure SQL | PostgreSQL Flexible |\n|--|-----------|---------------------|\n| Full backup | Weekly | Weekly |\n| Differential | 12-24 hours | Daily |\n| Transaction log | Every 5-10 min | Every 5 min |\n| Retention | 7 days (configurable 1-35) | 7 days (configurable 1-35) |\n| Geo-redundant | Optional | Optional |\n\n### Point-in-Time Restore (PITR)\n\n```powershell\n# Restore Azure SQL to a point in time (creates a NEW database)\naz sql db restore \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name myappdb \\\n  --dest-name myappdb-restored \\\n  --time \"2024-01-15T12:00:00\"\n\n# Restore PostgreSQL Flexible Server\naz postgres flexible-server restore \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --name pg-myapp-restored \\\n  --source-server pg-myapp-dev \\\n  --restore-time \"2024-01-15T12:00:00\"\n```\n\n### Manual Backup (Export)\n\n```powershell\n# Export Azure SQL to a .bacpac file in Blob Storage\naz sql db export \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --server sql-myapp-dev \\\n  --name myappdb \\\n  --storage-key-type StorageAccessKey \\\n  --storage-key $(az storage account keys list --account-name stmyappdev --query \"[0].value\" -o tsv) \\\n  --storage-uri \"https://stmyappdev.blob.core.windows.net/backups/myappdb.bacpac\" \\\n  --admin-user sqladmin \\\n  --admin-password <password>\n\n# PostgreSQL dump (from local machine)\npg_dump \"host=pg-myapp-dev.postgres.database.azure.com dbname=myappdb user=pgadmin\" \\\n  -f backup.sql\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Monitoring and Performance\n\n### Query Performance Insight (Azure SQL)\n\nIn Azure Portal: **SQL Database** > **Query Performance Insight**\n\nShows:\n- Top CPU-consuming queries\n- Long-running queries\n- Query wait statistics\n\n### pg_stat_statements (PostgreSQL)\n\n```sql\n-- Enable in PostgreSQL (requires pg_stat_statements extension)\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_stat_statements;\n\n-- Find slowest queries\nSELECT\n    calls,\n    mean_exec_time,\n    total_exec_time,\n    query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY mean_exec_time DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\n### Set Up Metric Alerts\n\n```powershell\n# Alert when CPU > 80% on Azure SQL\naz monitor metrics alert create \\\n  --name \"SQL-High-CPU\" \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --scopes $(az sql db show --resource-group rg-learning --server sql-myapp-dev --name myappdb --query id -o tsv) \\\n  --condition \"avg Percentage CPU > 80\" \\\n  --window-size 5m \\\n  --evaluation-frequency 1m \\\n  --action $(az monitor action-group show --name MyAlerts --resource-group rg-learning --query id -o tsv)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 29 — Choose Your Database\nBased on your mini-project, decide: Azure SQL or PostgreSQL?\nCreate the server and database. Connect from your local machine using a SQL client.\n\n### Day 30 — App Connection\nUpdate your app to connect to Azure database instead of the local Docker database.\nTest all CRUD operations work correctly with the Azure DB.\n\n### Day 31 — Managed Identity\nRemove the password from your connection string. Implement Managed Identity authentication.\nVerify the app still connects when deployed to App Service.\n\n### Day 32 — Migrations in Code\nSet up EF Core migrations (or Flyway). Create at least 3 migrations that represent your schema.\nRun `dotnet ef database update` and verify the tables are created.\n\n### Day 33 — Backup and Restore Practice\n1. Insert test data into your database\n2. Note the current timestamp\n3. Delete some records\n4. Perform a PITR to 1 minute before the delete\n5. Verify the records are back in the restored database\n\n### Day 34 — Monitoring\n1. Run some queries that are intentionally slow (full table scans)\n2. Find them in Query Performance Insight / pg_stat_statements\n3. Add an index and compare query time\n4. Set up a CPU alert\n\n### Day 35 — Self-Review\nSelf-demo: full data flow from app → Azure DB with Managed Identity, migration running on startup, monitoring dashboard visible.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Cheat Sheet\n\n```powershell\n# AZURE SQL\naz sql server create --name NAME --resource-group RG --admin-user USER --admin-password PASS\naz sql db create --server NAME --resource-group RG --name DB --edition GeneralPurpose\naz sql server firewall-rule create --server NAME --resource-group RG --name RULE --start-ip 0.0.0.0 --end-ip 0.0.0.0\n\n# POSTGRESQL\naz postgres flexible-server create --name NAME --resource-group RG --admin-user USER --admin-password PASS\naz postgres flexible-server db create --server-name NAME --resource-group RG --database-name DB\naz postgres flexible-server firewall-rule create --name SERVER --resource-group RG --rule-name RULE --start-ip 0.0.0.0 --end-ip 0.0.0.0\n\n# EF CORE MIGRATIONS\ndotnet ef migrations add NAME\ndotnet ef database update\ndotnet ef migrations script --idempotent -o script.sql\n\n# BACKUP / RESTORE\naz sql db restore --server NAME --resource-group RG --name SOURCE --dest-name DEST --time \"2024-01-15T12:00:00\"\naz postgres flexible-server restore --name NEW --source-server OLD --resource-group RG --restore-time \"2024-01-15T12:00:00\"\n```\n","06-azure-cicd":"# Phase 4 — Azure CI/CD\n\n> **Duration:** Days 36–56 (Weeks 6–8) | **Goal:** Automate build, test, and deployment with Azure DevOps Pipelines\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Azure DevOps Overview](#1-azure-devops-overview)\n2. [YAML Pipeline Fundamentals](#2-yaml-pipeline-fundamentals)\n3. [Build Pipeline](#3-build-pipeline)\n4. [Pipeline Agents](#4-pipeline-agents)\n5. [Variables and Secrets](#5-variables-and-secrets)\n6. [Artifacts](#6-artifacts)\n7. [Pipeline Templates](#7-pipeline-templates)\n8. [Environments and Approvals](#8-environments-and-approvals)\n9. [Deploy to App Service](#9-deploy-to-app-service)\n10. [Deploy to Container Apps](#10-deploy-to-container-apps)\n11. [Database Migrations in Pipeline](#11-database-migrations-in-pipeline)\n12. [Deployment Strategies](#12-deployment-strategies)\n13. [Quality Gates](#13-quality-gates)\n14. [Monitoring and Observability](#14-monitoring-and-observability)\n15. [Day-by-Day Exercises](#15-day-by-day-exercises)\n16. [Cheat Sheet](#16-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Azure DevOps Overview\n\nAzure DevOps is a suite of tools for the full software development lifecycle.\n\n### Services\n\n| Service | What it does |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Repos** | Git repository hosting (like GitHub) |\n| **Pipelines** | CI/CD automation |\n| **Boards** | Agile project management (issues, sprints) |\n| **Artifacts** | Package feeds (NuGet, npm, Maven, Docker) |\n| **Test Plans** | Manual and automated test management |\n\n### Setup\n\n```powershell\n# Login to Azure DevOps\naz devops login\n\n# Set your org as default\naz devops configure --defaults organization=https://dev.azure.com/yourorg\n\n# Create a project\naz devops project create --name myapp --visibility private\n\n# Set project as default\naz devops configure --defaults project=myapp\n```\n\n### Connect Azure DevOps to Azure (Service Connection)\n\nA **Service Connection** allows your pipeline to authenticate to Azure.\n\n1. Go to **Project Settings** > **Service Connections**\n2. Click **New Service Connection** > **Azure Resource Manager**\n3. Choose **Service Principal (automatic)**\n4. Select your subscription\n5. Name it: `azure-subscription-dev`\n\n---\n\n## 2. YAML Pipeline Fundamentals\n\n### Pipeline Structure\n\n```viz\npipeline-stages\n```\n\n```yaml\n# azure-pipelines.yml\n\ntrigger:              # when to run\n  - main\n\npool:                 # where to run\n  vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n\nstages:               # logical phases (build, test, deploy)\n  - stage: Build\n    jobs:             # units of work (can run parallel)\n      - job: BuildApp\n        steps:        # individual commands\n          - script: echo \"Hello\"\n          - task: NodeTool@0\n            inputs:\n              versionSpec: \"20.x\"\n```\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Concept | Scope | Runs on | Parallel? |\n|---------|-------|---------|-----------|\n| **Stage** | Major phase | N/A | Yes (if configured) |\n| **Job** | Unit of work | One agent | Yes (within a stage) |\n| **Step** | Single action | Same agent as job | No (sequential) |\n\n```viz\nstage-job-step\n```\n\n### Trigger Options\n\n```yaml\n# Run on push to main or develop\ntrigger:\n  branches:\n    include:\n      - main\n      - develop\n  paths:\n    exclude:\n      - docs/**\n      - \"*.md\"\n\n# Run on pull requests\npr:\n  branches:\n    include:\n      - main\n\n# Run on a schedule\nschedules:\n  - cron: \"0 2 * * *\"    # 2 AM daily\n    displayName: Nightly build\n    branches:\n      include:\n        - main\n    always: true           # run even if no code changes\n\n# Disable automatic triggers (manual only)\ntrigger: none\n```\n\n### Conditions\n\n```yaml\nsteps:\n  - script: echo \"Always runs\"\n\n  - script: echo \"Only on main\"\n    condition: eq(variables['Build.SourceBranch'], 'refs/heads/main')\n\n  - script: echo \"Only if previous step succeeded\"\n    condition: succeeded()\n\n  - script: echo \"Even if previous failed\"\n    condition: always()\n\n  - script: echo \"Only if build failed\"\n    condition: failed()\n```\n\n### Predefined Variables\n\n```yaml\n# Useful built-in variables\n$(Build.BuildId)           # unique build number\n$(Build.SourceBranch)      # refs/heads/main\n$(Build.SourceBranchName)  # main\n$(Build.Repository.Name)   # repo name\n$(System.DefaultWorkingDirectory)  # where your code is\n$(Agent.TempDirectory)     # temp dir on agent\n$(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)  # where to put artifacts\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Build Pipeline\n\n### Node.js Build Pipeline\n\n```yaml\ntrigger:\n  - main\n\npool:\n  vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n\nvariables:\n  nodeVersion: \"20.x\"\n\nstages:\n  - stage: Build\n    displayName: Build and Test\n    jobs:\n      - job: Build\n        steps:\n          - task: NodeTool@0\n            inputs:\n              versionSpec: $(nodeVersion)\n            displayName: Install Node.js\n\n          - script: npm ci\n            displayName: Install dependencies\n\n          - script: npm run lint\n            displayName: Lint\n\n          - script: npm test -- --ci --coverage\n            displayName: Unit tests\n\n          - task: PublishTestResults@2\n            inputs:\n              testResultsFormat: JUnit\n              testResultsFiles: \"**/test-results.xml\"\n            displayName: Publish test results\n\n          - task: PublishCodeCoverageResults@2\n            inputs:\n              codeCoverageTool: Cobertura\n              summaryFileLocation: \"**/coverage/cobertura-coverage.xml\"\n            displayName: Publish coverage\n\n          - script: npm run build\n            displayName: Build\n\n          - task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1\n            inputs:\n              PathtoPublish: dist\n              ArtifactName: app-dist\n            displayName: Publish artifact\n```\n\n### .NET Build Pipeline\n\n```yaml\ntrigger:\n  - main\n\npool:\n  vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n\nvariables:\n  buildConfiguration: Release\n\nstages:\n  - stage: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: Build\n        steps:\n          - task: UseDotNet@2\n            inputs:\n              version: \"8.x\"\n\n          - script: dotnet restore\n            displayName: Restore packages\n\n          - script: dotnet build --configuration $(buildConfiguration) --no-restore\n            displayName: Build\n\n          - script: dotnet test --configuration $(buildConfiguration) --no-build --logger trx --results-directory $(Agent.TempDirectory)\n            displayName: Test\n\n          - task: PublishTestResults@2\n            inputs:\n              testResultsFormat: VSTest\n              testResultsFiles: \"$(Agent.TempDirectory)/**/*.trx\"\n\n          - script: dotnet publish --configuration $(buildConfiguration) --output $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n            displayName: Publish\n\n          - task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1\n            inputs:\n              PathtoPublish: $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n              ArtifactName: app-publish\n```\n\n### Docker Build Pipeline\n\n```yaml\ntrigger:\n  - main\n\npool:\n  vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n\nvariables:\n  acrName: mydevopsacr\n  imageName: my-app\n  imageTag: $(Build.BuildId)\n\nstages:\n  - stage: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: BuildAndPush\n        steps:\n          - task: AzureCLI@2\n            inputs:\n              azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n              scriptType: bash\n              scriptLocation: inlineScript\n              inlineScript: |\n                az acr login --name $(acrName)\n                docker build -t $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag) .\n                docker build -t $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):latest .\n                docker push $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag)\n                docker push $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):latest\n            displayName: Build and push Docker image\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Pipeline Agents\n\nAgents are the **machines that execute your pipeline jobs**.\n\n### Microsoft-Hosted Agents\n\nAzure provides and maintains these. Fresh VM for every job.\n\n```yaml\npool:\n  vmImage: ubuntu-latest    # Ubuntu 22.04\n  # vmImage: windows-latest # Windows Server 2022\n  # vmImage: macos-latest   # macOS 14\n```\n\nPre-installed software: Node, .NET, Python, Docker, Azure CLI, kubectl, and many more.\n[Full list](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/agents/hosted)\n\n### Self-Hosted Agents\n\nRun on your own infrastructure. Use when:\n- You need specific software not on hosted agents\n- You need access to private networks\n- You have high volume (cost savings)\n- You need faster builds (persistent cache)\n\n```powershell\n# Register a self-hosted agent on your Windows machine\n# 1. In Azure DevOps: Organization Settings > Agent Pools > Add pool\n# 2. Download agent from Azure DevOps portal\n# 3. Extract and configure:\n.\\config.cmd --url https://dev.azure.com/yourorg `\n             --auth pat `\n             --token <your-pat> `\n             --pool Default `\n             --agent MyLocalAgent `\n             --runAsService\n```\n\n```yaml\n# Use self-hosted pool\npool:\n  name: Default          # pool name you created\n  demands:\n    - agent.os -equals Windows_NT\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Variables and Secrets\n\n### Variable Types\n\n| Type | Where | Use for |\n|------|-------|---------|\n| **Inline variables** | YAML file | Non-sensitive config |\n| **Variable group** | Azure DevOps Library | Shared config across pipelines |\n| **Pipeline secret** | Pipeline settings UI | Passwords, tokens |\n| **Key Vault linked** | Variable group linked to Key Vault | Production secrets |\n\n### Inline Variables\n\n```yaml\nvariables:\n  buildConfiguration: Release\n  nodeVersion: \"20.x\"\n  containerRegistry: myacr.azurecr.io\n\n  # Override per stage\n  - name: environment\n    value: dev\n```\n\n### Variable Groups\n\n```powershell\n# Create a variable group\naz pipelines variable-group create \\\n  --name \"myapp-dev-vars\" \\\n  --variables \\\n    NODE_ENV=development \\\n    API_URL=https://api-dev.myapp.com\n\n# Create a secret variable (masked in logs)\naz pipelines variable-group variable create \\\n  --group-id <id> \\\n  --name DATABASE_URL \\\n  --value \"postgres://...\" \\\n  --secret true\n```\n\n```yaml\n# Use in pipeline\nvariables:\n  - group: myapp-dev-vars\n\nsteps:\n  - script: echo $(NODE_ENV)          # prints: development\n  - script: echo $(DATABASE_URL)      # prints: *** (masked)\n```\n\n### Link Key Vault to Variable Group\n\n1. In Azure DevOps: **Pipelines** > **Library** > **+ Variable group**\n2. Toggle **Link secrets from an Azure key vault**\n3. Select your subscription and Key Vault\n4. Add secrets to sync\n\n```yaml\n# Reference Key Vault variables just like regular variables\nvariables:\n  - group: myapp-keyvault-secrets    # linked to Key Vault\n\nsteps:\n  - script: |\n      # $(DatabasePassword) comes from Key Vault, masked in logs\n      echo \"Connecting to database...\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Artifacts\n\nArtifacts are **outputs from a build** that you pass to later stages or store.\n\n### Publish and Download\n\n```yaml\nstages:\n  - stage: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: Build\n        steps:\n          - script: dotnet publish -o $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n          \n          - task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1\n            inputs:\n              PathtoPublish: $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n              ArtifactName: drop      # name of the artifact\n\n  - stage: Deploy\n    dependsOn: Build\n    jobs:\n      - deployment: DeployWeb\n        steps:\n          - task: DownloadBuildArtifacts@1\n            inputs:\n              buildType: current\n              artifactName: drop\n              downloadPath: $(System.DefaultWorkingDirectory)\n```\n\n### Pipeline Artifacts (Faster)\n\n```yaml\n# Publish (faster than BuildArtifacts)\n- task: PublishPipelineArtifact@1\n  inputs:\n    targetPath: $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n    artifact: drop\n\n# Download\n- task: DownloadPipelineArtifact@2\n  inputs:\n    artifact: drop\n    path: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Pipeline Templates\n\nTemplates let you **reuse YAML steps/jobs across pipelines**. Put shared templates in a separate repo or folder.\n\n### Step Template\n\n```yaml\n# templates/build-steps.yml\nparameters:\n  - name: nodeVersion\n    type: string\n    default: \"20.x\"\n\nsteps:\n  - task: NodeTool@0\n    inputs:\n      versionSpec: ${{ parameters.nodeVersion }}\n\n  - script: npm ci\n  - script: npm run lint\n  - script: npm test\n```\n\n```yaml\n# azure-pipelines.yml — use the template\nstages:\n  - stage: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: Build\n        steps:\n          - template: templates/build-steps.yml\n            parameters:\n              nodeVersion: \"20.x\"\n```\n\n### Job Template\n\n```yaml\n# templates/deploy-job.yml\nparameters:\n  - name: environment\n    type: string\n  - name: serviceConnection\n    type: string\n  - name: appName\n    type: string\n\njobs:\n  - deployment: Deploy_${{ parameters.environment }}\n    environment: ${{ parameters.environment }}\n    strategy:\n      runOnce:\n        deploy:\n          steps:\n            - task: AzureWebApp@1\n              inputs:\n                azureSubscription: ${{ parameters.serviceConnection }}\n                appName: ${{ parameters.appName }}\n```\n\n```yaml\n# azure-pipelines.yml\nstages:\n  - stage: DeployDev\n    jobs:\n      - template: templates/deploy-job.yml\n        parameters:\n          environment: dev\n          serviceConnection: azure-subscription-dev\n          appName: app-myapp-dev\n\n  - stage: DeployProd\n    jobs:\n      - template: templates/deploy-job.yml\n        parameters:\n          environment: prod\n          serviceConnection: azure-subscription-prod\n          appName: app-myapp-prod\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Environments and Approvals\n\n**Environments** in Azure DevOps represent deployment targets (dev, staging, prod) with optional protection rules.\n\n### Create Environment\n\n```powershell\naz devops environment create --name dev\naz devops environment create --name staging\naz devops environment create --name production\n```\n\n### Add Approval Gate\n\n1. In Azure DevOps: **Pipelines** > **Environments** > select `production`\n2. Click the **...** menu > **Approvals and checks**\n3. Click **+** > **Approvals**\n4. Add approvers (e.g., yourself or a colleague)\n5. Set timeout (e.g., 24 hours)\n\n### Use Environment in Pipeline\n\n```yaml\nstages:\n  - stage: DeployDev\n    jobs:\n      - deployment: Deploy\n        environment: dev           # no approval required\n        strategy:\n          runOnce:\n            deploy:\n              steps:\n                - script: echo \"Deploying to dev\"\n\n  - stage: DeployProd\n    dependsOn: DeployDev\n    jobs:\n      - deployment: Deploy\n        environment: production    # requires approval before running\n        strategy:\n          runOnce:\n            deploy:\n              steps:\n                - script: echo \"Deploying to production\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Deploy to App Service\n\n```yaml\n- stage: DeployAppService\n  jobs:\n    - deployment: Deploy\n      environment: dev\n      strategy:\n        runOnce:\n          deploy:\n            steps:\n              # Download artifact from build stage\n              - task: DownloadPipelineArtifact@2\n                inputs:\n                  artifact: drop\n\n              # Deploy to App Service\n              - task: AzureWebApp@1\n                inputs:\n                  azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n                  appType: webAppLinux\n                  appName: app-myapp-dev\n                  package: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n\n              # Or deploy a Docker container\n              - task: AzureWebAppContainer@1\n                inputs:\n                  azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n                  appName: app-myapp-dev\n                  containers: myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:$(Build.BuildId)\n```\n\n### Zero-Downtime with Deployment Slots\n\n```yaml\nsteps:\n  # Deploy to staging slot first\n  - task: AzureWebApp@1\n    inputs:\n      azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n      appName: app-myapp-dev\n      deployToSlotOrASE: true\n      resourceGroupName: rg-learning\n      slotName: staging\n      package: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n\n  # Run smoke tests against staging slot\n  - script: |\n      curl -f https://app-myapp-dev-staging.azurewebsites.net/health\n      echo \"Smoke test passed\"\n\n  # Swap staging to production\n  - task: AzureAppServiceManage@0\n    inputs:\n      azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n      Action: Swap Slots\n      WebAppName: app-myapp-dev\n      ResourceGroupName: rg-learning\n      SourceSlot: staging\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Deploy to Container Apps\n\n```yaml\n- stage: DeployContainerApps\n  jobs:\n    - deployment: Deploy\n      environment: dev\n      strategy:\n        runOnce:\n          deploy:\n            steps:\n              - task: AzureCLI@2\n                inputs:\n                  azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n                  scriptType: bash\n                  scriptLocation: inlineScript\n                  inlineScript: |\n                    az containerapp update \\\n                      --name api-myapp \\\n                      --resource-group rg-learning \\\n                      --image myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:$(Build.BuildId)\n\n                    # Verify the update\n                    az containerapp revision list \\\n                      --name api-myapp \\\n                      --resource-group rg-learning \\\n                      --output table\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11. Database Migrations in Pipeline\n\nRun migrations **before** deploying the new application version.\n\n```yaml\nstages:\n  - stage: Migrate\n    displayName: Database Migrations\n    dependsOn: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: RunMigrations\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        steps:\n          - task: UseDotNet@2\n            inputs:\n              version: \"8.x\"\n\n          - task: AzureCLI@2\n            inputs:\n              azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n              scriptType: bash\n              scriptLocation: inlineScript\n              inlineScript: |\n                # Get connection string from Key Vault\n                DB_CONN=$(az keyvault secret show \\\n                  --vault-name kv-myapp-dev \\\n                  --name DatabaseConnectionString \\\n                  --query value -o tsv)\n\n                # Run EF Core migrations\n                dotnet ef database update \\\n                  --connection \"$DB_CONN\" \\\n                  --project src/MyApp.Data \\\n                  --startup-project src/MyApp.Api\n            displayName: Run EF Core migrations\n\n  - stage: Deploy\n    displayName: Deploy Application\n    dependsOn: Migrate    # deploy only after migrations succeed\n    jobs:\n      - deployment: Deploy\n        environment: dev\n        strategy:\n          runOnce:\n            deploy:\n              steps:\n                - task: AzureWebApp@1\n                  inputs:\n                    azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n                    appName: app-myapp-dev\n                    package: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n```\n\n---\n\n## 12. Deployment Strategies\n\n### Blue/Green (via Slots)\n\n```viz\nblue-green\n```\n\n```\nSlot: production  (blue — live traffic)\nSlot: staging     (green — new version)\n\nDeploy to green → test → swap → green becomes blue\n```\n\nSee Section 9 for the swap implementation.\n\n### Canary (Container Apps)\n\n```viz\ncanary\n```\n\n```powershell\n# Create a new revision with 10% traffic\naz containerapp revision copy \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --image myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:v2\n\naz containerapp ingress traffic set \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --revision-weight previous=90 latest=10\n```\n\nGradually increase to 100% after validation.\n\n### Rolling Deployment (default in Container Apps)\n\n```yaml\n# In pipeline — Container Apps handles rolling by default\naz containerapp update \\\n  --name api-myapp \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --image myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:$(Build.BuildId)\n# New instances start before old ones stop\n```\n\n### Rollback\n\n```yaml\n# Rollback App Service to previous deployment\n- task: AzureAppServiceManage@0\n  inputs:\n    azureSubscription: azure-subscription-dev\n    Action: Start Azure App Service\n    WebAppName: app-myapp-dev\n\n# Rollback Container App to previous revision\n- task: AzureCLI@2\n  inputs:\n    scriptType: bash\n    inlineScript: |\n      PREV_REVISION=$(az containerapp revision list \\\n        --name api-myapp \\\n        --resource-group rg-learning \\\n        --query \"[1].name\" -o tsv)\n\n      az containerapp ingress traffic set \\\n        --name api-myapp \\\n        --resource-group rg-learning \\\n        --revision-weight $PREV_REVISION=100\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13. Quality Gates\n\n### Unit Tests Must Pass\n\n```yaml\n- script: npm test -- --ci\n  displayName: Unit tests\n\n- task: PublishTestResults@2\n  condition: always()    # publish results even if tests failed\n  inputs:\n    testResultsFormat: JUnit\n    testResultsFiles: \"**/junit.xml\"\n    failTaskOnFailedTests: true   # FAIL the pipeline if any test fails\n```\n\n### Security Scanning (Trivy — Docker images)\n\n```yaml\n- script: |\n    docker run --rm \\\n      -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \\\n      aquasec/trivy image \\\n      --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \\\n      --exit-code 1 \\\n      myacr.azurecr.io/my-app:$(Build.BuildId)\n  displayName: Security scan (Trivy)\n  # Pipeline fails if HIGH or CRITICAL vulnerabilities found\n```\n\n### OWASP Dependency Check\n\n```yaml\n- task: dependency-check-build-task@6\n  inputs:\n    projectName: MyApp\n    scanPath: \"$(System.DefaultWorkingDirectory)\"\n    format: HTML\n    failOnCVSS: \"7\"      # fail on CVSS score >= 7\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14. Monitoring and Observability\n\n### Application Insights\n\n```powershell\n# Create Application Insights\naz monitor app-insights component create \\\n  --app appi-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --location eastus \\\n  --kind web\n\n# Get instrumentation key\naz monitor app-insights component show \\\n  --app appi-myapp-dev \\\n  --resource-group rg-learning \\\n  --query instrumentationKey \\\n  --output tsv\n```\n\n### Instrument Your App (C#)\n\n```powershell\ndotnet add package Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore\n```\n\n```csharp\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services.AddApplicationInsightsTelemetry(\n    builder.Configuration[\"APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING\"]\n);\n```\n\n### Instrument Your App (Node.js)\n\n```powershell\nnpm install applicationinsights\n```\n\n```typescript\nimport appInsights from \"applicationinsights\";\n\nappInsights\n  .setup(process.env.APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING)\n  .setAutoDependencyCorrelation(true)\n  .setAutoCollectRequests(true)\n  .setAutoCollectExceptions(true)\n  .start();\n```\n\n### Useful KQL Queries\n\n```kusto\n// Requests in the last hour\nrequests\n| where timestamp > ago(1h)\n| summarize count() by resultCode\n| order by count_ desc\n\n// Failed requests\nrequests\n| where success == false\n| project timestamp, name, resultCode, duration\n| order by timestamp desc\n| take 50\n\n// Slowest endpoints (p95 latency)\nrequests\n| summarize percentile(duration, 95) by name\n| order by percentile_duration_95 desc\n| take 10\n\n// Exceptions in last 24h\nexceptions\n| where timestamp > ago(24h)\n| summarize count() by type, outerMessage\n| order by count_ desc\n\n// Average response time over time\nrequests\n| where timestamp > ago(6h)\n| summarize avg(duration) by bin(timestamp, 5m)\n| render timechart\n```\n\n---\n\n## 15. Day-by-Day Exercises\n\n### Day 36 — Azure DevOps Setup\n1. Create an Azure DevOps organization and project\n2. Push your mini-project code to Azure Repos (or connect GitHub)\n3. Create a Service Connection to your Azure subscription\n\n### Day 37 — First Pipeline\nWrite a `azure-pipelines.yml` that:\n- Triggers on push to `main`\n- Installs dependencies\n- Runs tests\n- Prints \"Build complete\"\n\n### Day 38 — Self-hosted Agent\nRegister your local machine as a self-hosted agent. Run your pipeline on it. Observe the difference vs hosted agents.\n\n### Day 39 — Secrets in Pipeline\nCreate a variable group in Library. Add your DB connection string as a secret. Reference it in your pipeline.\n\n### Day 40 — Artifacts\nPublish your built app as a pipeline artifact. In a second job, download and inspect it.\n\n### Day 41 — Templates\nExtract your build steps into a reusable template file. Have two separate pipelines both use the same template.\n\n### Day 42 — Review\nSelf-review of your YAML pipelines. Discuss: what would break if you pushed bad code? How does the pipeline catch it?\n\n### Day 43 — Environments + Approvals\nCreate `dev` and `production` environments. Add yourself as approver on production. Trigger a deploy and approve it.\n\n### Day 44 — Deploy to App Service\nAdd a deploy stage to your pipeline. Deploy your app to App Service. Verify it's running at the live URL.\n\n### Day 45 — Deploy to Container Apps\nDeploy your Docker image to Container Apps from the pipeline. Use `$(Build.BuildId)` as the image tag.\n\n### Day 46 — Migrations in Pipeline\nAdd a migration stage before the deploy stage. Verify the order: build → migrate → deploy.\n\n### Day 47 — Deployment Slots\nImplement blue/green with App Service slots. Deploy to staging, run a smoke test, then swap.\n\n### Day 48 — Rollback\nDeliberately deploy a broken version. Practice rolling back to the previous working version.\n\n### Day 49 — Full CD Review\nEnd-to-end demo: push a commit → pipeline builds → tests run → migrations run → staging deploy → approval → production deploy.\n\n### Day 50 — Tests Must Pass\nBreak your tests intentionally. Verify the pipeline fails and does NOT deploy. Fix and push again.\n\n### Day 51 — Security Scanning\nAdd Trivy scanning to your Docker build stage. Introduce a vulnerable package, observe the failure, fix it.\n\n### Day 52 — Application Insights\nAdd Application Insights to your app. Make some requests. View them in Azure Portal.\n\n### Day 53 — KQL Queries\nWrite the 5 KQL queries in Section 14. Save them as queries in your Log Analytics workspace.\n\n### Day 54 — Alerts\nSet up: (1) alert on 5xx errors > 5/min, (2) alert on response time > 2 seconds p95.\n\n### Day 55 — Cost Management\nTag all resources with `environment=dev` and `project=myapp`. Create a budget of $50/month with email notification at 80%.\n\n### Day 56 — Final Review\nFull monitoring dashboard demo. Walk through: a request, its traces, the logs, and the metrics.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Cheat Sheet\n\n```yaml\n# PIPELINE TRIGGERS\ntrigger:\n  branches:\n    include: [main, develop]\npr:\n  branches:\n    include: [main]\ntrigger: none    # manual only\n\n# COMMON TASKS\n- task: NodeTool@0\n  inputs: { versionSpec: \"20.x\" }\n\n- task: UseDotNet@2\n  inputs: { version: \"8.x\" }\n\n- task: AzureCLI@2\n  inputs:\n    azureSubscription: my-service-connection\n    scriptType: bash\n    scriptLocation: inlineScript\n    inlineScript: az --version\n\n- task: AzureWebApp@1\n  inputs:\n    azureSubscription: my-service-connection\n    appName: my-app\n    package: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n\n- task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1\n  inputs:\n    PathtoPublish: $(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)\n    ArtifactName: drop\n\n- task: DownloadPipelineArtifact@2\n  inputs:\n    artifact: drop\n    path: $(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop\n\n# VARIABLES\nvariables:\n  myVar: value\n  - group: my-variable-group\n\n# CONDITIONS\ncondition: succeeded()\ncondition: failed()\ncondition: always()\ncondition: eq(variables['Build.SourceBranch'], 'refs/heads/main')\n\n# DEPLOYMENT JOB\n- deployment: Deploy\n  environment: production    # triggers approval if configured\n  strategy:\n    runOnce:\n      deploy:\n        steps:\n          - script: echo \"Deploying\"\n```\n","07-capstone":"# Phase 5 — Capstone Project\n\n> **Duration:** Days 57–63 (Week 9) | **Goal:** Build and deploy a complete production-grade system end-to-end\n\n## Overview\n\nThis capstone ties together everything from all previous phases into a single, deployable application with full CI/CD, monitoring, and security.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                          Azure DevOps                                        │\n│                                                                              │\n│  Git Push ──> Pipelines ──> Build ──> Test ──> Migrate ──> Deploy (Staging) │\n│                                                    │                         │\n│                                              Manual Approval                 │\n│                                                    │                         │\n│                                             Deploy (Production)              │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                      │\n                          ┌───────────┴────────────┐\n                          ▼                        ▼\n              ┌───────────────────┐    ┌────────────────────┐\n              │  Azure Container  │    │  Azure Container   │\n              │  Apps (Staging)   │    │  Apps (Production) │\n              │  api-myapp-stg    │    │  api-myapp-prod    │\n              └────────┬──────────┘    └────────┬───────────┘\n                       │                        │\n              ┌────────▼──────────────────────── ▼─────────┐\n              │              Azure VNet                      │\n              │                                              │\n              │  ┌──────────────┐   ┌────────────────────┐  │\n              │  │ Azure Key    │   │ Azure PostgreSQL    │  │\n              │  │ Vault        │   │ Flexible Server     │  │\n              │  │ (secrets)    │   │ (private subnet)   │  │\n              │  └──────────────┘   └────────────────────┘  │\n              │                                              │\n              │  ┌──────────────┐   ┌────────────────────┐  │\n              │  │ Azure Blob   │   │ Application        │  │\n              │  │ Storage      │   │ Insights           │  │\n              │  │ (files)      │   │ (monitoring)       │  │\n              │  └──────────────┘   └────────────────────┘  │\n              └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                         │\n              ┌──────────▼───────────┐\n              │  Microsoft Entra ID  │\n              │  (authentication)    │\n              └──────────────────────┘\n              ┌──────────────────────┐\n              │  Azure Communication │\n              │  Services (email)    │\n              └──────────────────────┘\n              ┌──────────────────────┐\n              │  Azure Container     │\n              │  Registry (images)   │\n              └──────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Application Requirements\n\nBuild a **Task Management API** (or any CRUD app you prefer) with these features:\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n| Feature | Component Used |\n|---------|---------------|\n| User registration | PostgreSQL + email welcome (ACS) |\n| User login | Entra ID OAuth 2.0 |\n| Create/Read/Update/Delete tasks | PostgreSQL |\n| Assign tasks to users | PostgreSQL |\n| Upload task attachments | Blob Storage |\n| Email notification on task assignment | ACS |\n| Admin-only endpoints | Entra ID App Roles (RBAC) |\n\n### Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| Requirement | How |\n|------------|-----|\n| No passwords in code | Key Vault + Managed Identity |\n| HTTPS only | Container Apps handles this automatically |\n| Health endpoint | `/health` returns 200 when DB is reachable |\n| Structured logging | Application Insights |\n| Automated deploys | Azure DevOps Pipeline |\n| Zero-downtime deploys | Container Apps rolling update |\n| Schema migrations | EF Core / Flyway, runs before app deploy |\n\n---\n\n## Complete Pipeline File\n\n```yaml\n# azure-pipelines.yml\n\ntrigger:\n  branches:\n    include:\n      - main\n\nvariables:\n  - group: myapp-prod-secrets          # Key Vault linked variable group\n  - name: acrName\n    value: mydevopsacr\n  - name: imageName\n    value: task-api\n  - name: imageTag\n    value: $(Build.BuildId)\n\nstages:\n\n  # ─── STAGE 1: BUILD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  - stage: Build\n    displayName: Build and Test\n    jobs:\n      - job: Build\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        steps:\n          - task: UseDotNet@2\n            inputs:\n              version: \"8.x\"\n\n          - script: dotnet restore\n            displayName: Restore\n\n          - script: dotnet build --configuration Release --no-restore\n            displayName: Build\n\n          - script: |\n              dotnet test --configuration Release --no-build \\\n                --logger trx \\\n                --results-directory $(Agent.TempDirectory)/testresults\n            displayName: Unit Tests\n\n          - task: PublishTestResults@2\n            condition: always()\n            inputs:\n              testResultsFormat: VSTest\n              testResultsFiles: $(Agent.TempDirectory)/testresults/**/*.trx\n              failTaskOnFailedTests: true\n\n          - task: AzureCLI@2\n            displayName: Build and push Docker image\n            inputs:\n              azureSubscription: azure-subscription-prod\n              scriptType: bash\n              scriptLocation: inlineScript\n              inlineScript: |\n                az acr login --name $(acrName)\n\n                docker build \\\n                  -t $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag) \\\n                  -t $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):latest \\\n                  .\n\n                # Security scan before push\n                docker run --rm \\\n                  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \\\n                  aquasec/trivy image \\\n                  --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \\\n                  --exit-code 1 \\\n                  $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag)\n\n                docker push $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag)\n                docker push $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):latest\n\n  # ─── STAGE 2: DEPLOY TO STAGING ──────────────────────────────────────────\n  - stage: DeployStaging\n    displayName: Deploy to Staging\n    dependsOn: Build\n    jobs:\n      - job: Migrate\n        displayName: Run DB Migrations\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        steps:\n          - task: UseDotNet@2\n            inputs:\n              version: \"8.x\"\n\n          - task: AzureCLI@2\n            displayName: Run EF Core migrations\n            inputs:\n              azureSubscription: azure-subscription-prod\n              scriptType: bash\n              scriptLocation: inlineScript\n              inlineScript: |\n                DB_CONN=$(az keyvault secret show \\\n                  --vault-name kv-myapp-prod \\\n                  --name StagingDbConnectionString \\\n                  --query value -o tsv)\n\n                dotnet ef database update \\\n                  --connection \"$DB_CONN\" \\\n                  --project src/MyApp.Data \\\n                  --startup-project src/MyApp.Api\n\n      - deployment: Deploy\n        displayName: Deploy App\n        dependsOn: Migrate\n        environment: staging\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        strategy:\n          runOnce:\n            deploy:\n              steps:\n                - task: AzureCLI@2\n                  displayName: Update Container App\n                  inputs:\n                    azureSubscription: azure-subscription-prod\n                    scriptType: bash\n                    scriptLocation: inlineScript\n                    inlineScript: |\n                      az containerapp update \\\n                        --name api-myapp-stg \\\n                        --resource-group rg-myapp-prod \\\n                        --image $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag)\n\n                - script: |\n                    APP_URL=$(az containerapp show \\\n                      --name api-myapp-stg \\\n                      --resource-group rg-myapp-prod \\\n                      --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn \\\n                      -o tsv)\n\n                    echo \"Running smoke tests against https://$APP_URL\"\n                    curl -f \"https://$APP_URL/health\"\n                    echo \"Smoke tests passed\"\n                  displayName: Smoke tests\n\n  # ─── STAGE 3: DEPLOY TO PRODUCTION ───────────────────────────────────────\n  - stage: DeployProduction\n    displayName: Deploy to Production\n    dependsOn: DeployStaging\n    jobs:\n      - job: Migrate\n        displayName: Run DB Migrations\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        steps:\n          - task: AzureCLI@2\n            displayName: Run EF Core migrations (prod)\n            inputs:\n              azureSubscription: azure-subscription-prod\n              scriptType: bash\n              scriptLocation: inlineScript\n              inlineScript: |\n                DB_CONN=$(az keyvault secret show \\\n                  --vault-name kv-myapp-prod \\\n                  --name ProdDbConnectionString \\\n                  --query value -o tsv)\n\n                dotnet ef database update \\\n                  --connection \"$DB_CONN\" \\\n                  --project src/MyApp.Data \\\n                  --startup-project src/MyApp.Api\n\n      - deployment: Deploy\n        displayName: Deploy App to Production\n        dependsOn: Migrate\n        environment: production      # requires manual approval\n        pool:\n          vmImage: ubuntu-latest\n        strategy:\n          runOnce:\n            deploy:\n              steps:\n                - task: AzureCLI@2\n                  displayName: Update Container App (Production)\n                  inputs:\n                    azureSubscription: azure-subscription-prod\n                    scriptType: bash\n                    scriptLocation: inlineScript\n                    inlineScript: |\n                      az containerapp update \\\n                        --name api-myapp-prod \\\n                        --resource-group rg-myapp-prod \\\n                        --image $(acrName).azurecr.io/$(imageName):$(imageTag)\n\n                - script: |\n                    APP_URL=$(az containerapp show \\\n                      --name api-myapp-prod \\\n                      --resource-group rg-myapp-prod \\\n                      --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn \\\n                      -o tsv)\n\n                    curl -f \"https://$APP_URL/health\"\n                    echo \"Production deployment verified\"\n                  displayName: Production smoke test\n```\n\n---\n\n## Infrastructure Provisioning Script\n\nRun this once to set up all Azure resources before the first deploy.\n\n```powershell\n# infra/provision.ps1\n\n$RG = \"rg-myapp-prod\"\n$LOCATION = \"eastus\"\n$ACR = \"mydevopsacr\"\n$KV = \"kv-myapp-prod\"\n$PG = \"pg-myapp-prod\"\n$STG = \"stmyappprod\"\n$ACS = \"acs-myapp-prod\"\n$APPI = \"appi-myapp-prod\"\n$VNET = \"vnet-myapp-prod\"\n$ENV = \"env-myapp-prod\"\n\n# Resource group\naz group create --name $RG --location $LOCATION\n\n# Container Registry\naz acr create --name $ACR --resource-group $RG --sku Basic\n\n# Key Vault\naz keyvault create --name $KV --resource-group $RG --location $LOCATION\n\n# Virtual Network\naz network vnet create --name $VNET --resource-group $RG --address-prefix 10.0.0.0/16\naz network vnet subnet create --name subnet-app --resource-group $RG --vnet-name $VNET --address-prefix 10.0.1.0/24\naz network vnet subnet create --name subnet-data --resource-group $RG --vnet-name $VNET --address-prefix 10.0.2.0/24\n\n# PostgreSQL (private)\naz postgres flexible-server create `\n  --name $PG `\n  --resource-group $RG `\n  --location $LOCATION `\n  --admin-user pgadmin `\n  --admin-password \"ChangeMe123!\" `\n  --sku-name Standard_B1ms `\n  --tier Burstable `\n  --version 16 `\n  --public-access None\n\n# Blob Storage\naz storage account create --name $STG --resource-group $RG --sku Standard_LRS --kind StorageV2\naz storage container create --account-name $STG --name uploads --public-access off\n\n# Application Insights\naz monitor app-insights component create --app $APPI --resource-group $RG --location $LOCATION --kind web\n\n# Container Apps Environment\naz containerapp env create --name $ENV --resource-group $RG --location $LOCATION\n\n# Container Apps (Staging + Production)\naz containerapp create `\n  --name api-myapp-stg `\n  --resource-group $RG `\n  --environment $ENV `\n  --image nginx `\n  --target-port 8080 `\n  --ingress external `\n  --min-replicas 0 `\n  --max-replicas 3\n\naz containerapp create `\n  --name api-myapp-prod `\n  --resource-group $RG `\n  --environment $ENV `\n  --image nginx `\n  --target-port 8080 `\n  --ingress external `\n  --min-replicas 1 `\n  --max-replicas 10\n\n# ACS Email\naz communication create --name $ACS --resource-group $RG --location global --data-location unitedstates\n\nWrite-Host \"Infrastructure provisioned successfully.\"\nWrite-Host \"Next: populate Key Vault secrets and configure Managed Identities.\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Checklist\n\n### Infrastructure\n- [ ] Resource group created with consistent naming\n- [ ] All resources tagged: `environment=prod`, `project=myapp`\n- [ ] VNet created with app and data subnets\n- [ ] PostgreSQL in private subnet (no public access)\n- [ ] Key Vault holds all secrets (no passwords in app config)\n- [ ] Managed Identity enabled on Container Apps\n- [ ] ACR created and accessible from pipeline\n- [ ] Application Insights connected\n\n### Application\n- [ ] `/health` endpoint returns 200 when DB is reachable\n- [ ] Structured logging to Application Insights\n- [ ] All secrets read from Key Vault (via Managed Identity)\n- [ ] DB connection uses Managed Identity (no password)\n- [ ] OAuth 2.0 login with Entra ID\n- [ ] At least one RBAC-protected endpoint\n- [ ] Email sends on user registration\n- [ ] File upload stores to Blob Storage\n- [ ] Migrations run on startup (or in pipeline before deploy)\n\n### Pipeline\n- [ ] Triggers on push to `main`\n- [ ] Unit tests run and FAIL the pipeline if they fail\n- [ ] Docker image built and pushed to ACR with `$(Build.BuildId)` tag\n- [ ] Security scan (Trivy) runs before push\n- [ ] Migration stage runs BEFORE deploy stage\n- [ ] Staging deploy happens automatically\n- [ ] Production deploy requires manual approval\n- [ ] Smoke test runs after each deploy\n- [ ] Pipeline uses variable groups — no hardcoded secrets in YAML\n\n### Monitoring\n- [ ] Live requests visible in Application Insights\n- [ ] Exceptions captured and searchable\n- [ ] KQL queries saved for: top errors, slow endpoints, request volume\n- [ ] Alert: 5xx rate > 5/min\n- [ ] Alert: Response time p95 > 2s\n- [ ] Budget alert at 80% of $50/month\n\n### Security\n- [ ] No secrets committed to git (`git log --all -S \"password\"` returns nothing)\n- [ ] PostgreSQL not publicly accessible\n- [ ] Container Apps only expose HTTPS (port 443)\n- [ ] NSG rules reviewed\n- [ ] App Roles defined and enforced\n\n---\n\n## Final Demo Script (Day 63)\n\nWalk through these in order yourself (record a demo or check off each step):\n\n1. **Architecture overview** — draw the diagram from memory and explain each component\n2. **Push a code change** — make a small visible change (e.g., change a response message)\n3. **Watch the pipeline** — show all stages running in Azure DevOps\n4. **Approve production** — approve the deployment gate\n5. **Verify the change** — call the production API, show the change is live\n6. **Show monitoring** — open Application Insights, find the request you just made\n7. **Show security** — demonstrate that `/admin` returns 403 without the admin role\n8. **Show email** — trigger a user registration, show the welcome email arrives\n9. **Explain a rollback** — describe exactly what you would do if production broke\n\n---\n\n## Questions to Ask Yourself\n\n1. What happens if the migration fails halfway through? How do you recover?\n2. How do you rotate a secret in Key Vault without downtime?\n3. If your Container App is getting 500 errors, what's your first step?\n4. How would you add a new developer to this project securely?\n5. What would you change if this needed to handle 10x more traffic?\n6. How do you know when production is broken before your users report it?\n7. What's the difference between a deployment slot swap and a Container Apps revision?\n8. If a security vulnerability is found in a base Docker image, how do you remediate it?\n"}
